ce naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time.\"\n\nSo the evaluator will parse the `x-plumbline` source, extract file and line, read that line of that file, and check the time appears there. So we need the `x-plumbline` to include something like `{\"file\": \"custody/evidence/harness-maker-....log\", \"line\": 42}` or a string `\"harness-maker-....log:42\"`.\n\nSince the evaluator will read the line, for a JSON Lines log the line number is the physical line. For a JSON list (indented), the `sent_at` appears on some line. Hmm.\n\nActually let me re-read: \"each time with an `x-plumbline` source naming file and line.\" And N3: \"Every time field in the record (`frozenAt`, `startedAt`, `producedAt`, `checks[].at`, approvals, disposition) has an `x-plumbline` source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time. Any time without a source fails.\"\n\nSo for every time field, the record's `x-plumbline` must name file+line. That includes `frozenAt`, approvals, disposition (from `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`), and `checks[].at` (from harness logs).\n\nThis is a bigger change. Let me think about how to structure it.\n\nWe need:\n1. A helper to record file+line for a value in a file.\n2. For the events log, we know the line number as we parse it (enumerate lines).\n3. For harness logs, we need to find the line where `sent_at` / `received_at` appear.\n\nFor harness logs, the format is JSON (list or single object) or JSON Lines. We need line numbers. We could compute line numbers by finding the value in the raw text. E.g., search for `\"sent_at\": \"<value>\"` and record the line. That's a bit hacky but works for JSON lists (indented) and JSON Lines.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: the `Call` class could carry the raw text and we search for the timestamp string.\n\nLet me design:\n\nAdd to `Call`:\n- `self.sent_at` = parse `raw.get(\"sent_at\")` to datetime\n- `self.received_at` = parse `raw.get(\"received_at\")` to d