at if `response.provider` is present? That's not \"routing config or provider pins\" — it's the response. So maybe we can still use `response.provider` + pin host? Hmm, but the host comes from the pin. So the endpoint `pin.host` is derived from the pin. The `response.provider` only confirms the provider name.\n\nActually, the pin is the only source of the host. So any endpoint derived for an aggregator call comes from the pin. The question is whether the log confirms it. `served_by` confirms from response.provider / metadata / generation. If generation is null but response.provider present, the provider is confirmed but not by the generation reply.\n\nThe task says \"when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply, never derive an `endpoint` from routing config or provider pins\". This suggests: if generation is null, the endpoint must not be written (or the record must be refused). Even if response.provider is present.\n\nHmm, that's stricter. Let me interpret: the generation reply is the CL1-15 evidence. Without it, the endpoint is not evidenced. So we should either refuse or mark the endpoint absent.\n\nLet me go with: if `generation` is null/missing for a call, refuse (exit 3) with a named error naming the pass. That satisfies N4(a). But does it break N5? N5 has all replies present, so no refusal. Good.\n\nBut wait — the fixture `job-rejected` (base) presumably has generation replies present. If I add a refusal for null generation, and the base fixture has null generation somewhere, tests might break. Let me check the base fixture... I don't have the files. Hmm. The task says `tests/validate_schemas.py` (112 cases) must still pass. If I make null generation a refusal, and a base fixture has null generation, a test expecting success would fail.\n\nBut the task also says \"The evaluator runs its own held-out fixture jobs (`single-call`, `maker-10min`, `genreply-null`, `genreply-full`)\". So `genreply-null` is new. The base `job-rejected` likely has generation present. Let me assume so.\n\nHmm, but there's a risk. Let me look at the base `custody_history.py` `endpoint_of`:\n```python\nif not c.served:\n    ref.add(\"endpoint\", f\"{who}: {c.label} went to {c.host} and the log does not say which provider served it ...\")\n    return None, route\n```\n\nSo if no `served` info at all, it already refuses. `served` includes generation. So if generation is null AND no response.provider/metadata, it refuses. But if response.provider is present, it proceeds.\n\nSo the current code already handles the \"no evidence at all\" case. The task wants to additionally handle \"generation null but response.provider present\" by not deriving endpoint from the pin.\n\nHmm, let me think about the fixture `genreply-null`. It's \"a model pass whose `/api/v1/generation` reply is missing/null\". If the response still has `provider`, the current code would derive the endpoint. The task says this is wrong.\n\nSo I need to add: if `generation` is null, do not derive endpoint. Options: refuse, or leave endpoint absent with a flag.\n\nLet me go with refusal for simplicity and clarity. But wait — N4 says \"refuse with a named error OR record the CL1-15 evidence absent\". If I refuse, the whole record isn't written, so the evaluator can't check the record. But N4 says \"either (a) exits non-zero with a named error, or (b) records...\". So (a) is acceptable; the evaluator checks the exit code and stderr.\n\nOK, let me implement refusal. In `evaluator_history`, after `annotate`, check each call's `generation`:\n```python\nfor c in calls:\n    if c.raw.get(\"generation\") is None and \"generation\" in c.raw:\n        ref.add(\"generation\", f\"{who}: {c.label} has no /api/v1/generation reply (generation is null); the endpoint it was served by is not evidenced, so no endpoint is written (bar N4)\")\n```\n\nHmm, but what if the log format doesn't include a `generation` key at all (e.g., direct provider calls, not OpenRouter)? For direct provider calls, there's