reply; the endpoint is not evidenced\")`.\n\nBut wait — the refusal means nothing is written. N4 says \"either (a) exits non-zero with a named error\". Exit 3 with a named error qualifies.\n\nBut N5 requires `genreply-full` to pass. If all replies present, no refusal.\n\nAnd the \"endpoint\" derivation: if we refuse when generation is null, we never write an endpoint for that pass. Good.\n\nBut hold on — does the maker also need generation? N4 says \"a model pass\". The evaluator passes. But the maker also makes calls with generation. Hmm. Let me apply to both maker and evaluator to be safe. Actually, N4 is about \"that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence\". So evaluator passes. But the maker's endpoint also comes from a call. Let me think.\n\nActually, the maker's `endpoint` also comes from `last.endpoint`, derived via `endpoint_of`. If the maker's last call has no generation, and we derive endpoint from the pin (because `response.provider` present), is that a problem? The task says \"when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply\". The maker isn't a \"pass\". But the spirit is: don't derive endpoint without evidence.\n\nHmm. Let me be conservative and apply the rule to both maker and evaluator: if a call's `generation` is null (or missing), don't derive an endpoint from the pin for that call; instead, if `response.provider`/metadata is present, we can still use it? The task says \"never derive an endpoint from routing config or provider pins\". If `response.provider` is present, the endpoint is derived from the log, not from the pin alone. But the pin gives the host. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me re-read N6: \"The generation-reply code path in `kit/emit_custody.py` has no fallback that derives an `endpoint` from routing config or provider pins.\"\n\n\"generation-reply code path\" — the code that handles the generation reply. If the generation reply is null, there should be no fallback to the pin. So: when `generation` is null, do not use the pin to set the endpoint.\n\nBut what if `response.provider` is present? That's not \"routing config or provider pins\" — it's the response. So maybe we can still use `response.provider` + pin host? Hmm, but the host comes from the pin. So the endpoint `pin.host` is derived from the pin. The `response.provider` only confirms the provider name.\n\nActually, the pin is the only source of the host. So any endpoint derived for an aggregator call comes from the pin. The question is whether the log confirms it. `served_by` confirms from response.provider / metadata / generation. If generation is null but response.provider present, the provider is confirmed but not by the generation reply.\n\nThe task says \"when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply, never derive an `endpoint` from routing config or provider pins\". This suggests: if generation is null, the endpoint must not be written (or the record must be refused). Even if response.provider is present.\n\nHmm, that's stricter. Let me interpret: the generation reply is the CL1-15 evidence. Without it, the endpoint is not evidenced. So we should either refuse or mark the endpoint absent.\n\nLet me go with: if `generation` is null/missing for a call, refuse (exit 3) with a named error naming the pass. That satisfies N4(a). But does it break N5? N5 has all replies present, so no refusal. Good.\n\nBut wait — the fixture `job-rejected` (base) presumably has generation replies present. If I add a refusal for null generation, and the base fixture has null generation somewhere, tests might break. Let me check the base fixture... I don't have the files. Hmm. The task says `tests/validate_schemas.py` (112 cases) must still pass. If I make null generation a refusal, and a base fixture has null generation, a test expecting success would fail.\n\nBut the task also says \"The evaluator runs its own held-out fixture jobs (`single-call`, `maker-10min`, `genreply-null`, `genreply-full`)\". So `genreply-null` is new. The base `job-rejected` likely has generation pre