t, then generation check. Both refusals coexist.\n\nBut wait, `annotate` calls `endpoint_of` which sets `c.endpoint = pin.host`. Then my generation check refuses. The record isn't written, so `c.endpoint` doesn't matter.\n\nHmm, but if I refuse, the test for `genreply-null` expects exit 3. Good.\n\nBut what about the base `job-rejected`? If its logs have `generation: null`, my check would refuse, breaking the base test. Let me check the likelihood. The base fixture was created before the generation-reply requirement. It might use `response.provider` only, with `generation: null` or absent.\n\nHmm, this is risky. Let me look at the task's fixture list again: \"The evaluator runs its own held-out fixture jobs (`single-call`, `maker-10min`, `genreply-null`, `genreply-full`)\". So these are new fixtures, not the base ones. The base `job-rejected` is used by `tests/validate_schemas.py`. If I break it, the base suite fails.\n\nLet me check: does `job-rejected` have harness logs? The task says \"Fixtures are run by S1 against the Maker's revision of the emitter. 'Fixture job' = a job dir under `tests/fixtures/custody/` with `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`, harness logs and an `inputs/custody.json`.\" So yes, `job-rejected` has harness logs.\n\nThe base code `custody_history.py` `served_by` reads `generation` if present. The base fixture likely has `generation` present (since the code was written to read it). Let me assume it's a dict.\n\nActually, let me look at the failure fixture `routing-unpinned`: \"the request's `provider.order` lists two slugs with no `allow_fallbacks:false` and no ZDR. The record is written with gap `routing.maker.request`\". So this fixture's record IS written (exit 1), meaning the endpoint is derived. If generation is null there, my refusal would prevent the record from being written, breaking the test. But `routing-unpinned` is about the routing request, not generation. It likely has generation present.\n\nOK, I'll take the risk and add the check. 