t break an exact-kind assertion.\n\nTo be safe: only add the generation refusal when it would *otherwise* be used, i.e. after confirming served-by and pin match — i.e., after the `if not c.served` and `other` checks. In pin-provider-missing, we return earlier at `not c.served`. In pin-fallback, return at `other`. In routing-unpinned, served by pin & generation present presumably. In pin-late, order refusal + endpoint resolution fine.\n\nSo place my generation-null check right before the final `route.update(...)` / `return pin.get(\"host\")` — after served-by confirmed and no fallback. Then only calls that WOULD get a pin-derived endpoint are affected. \n\nFor N4 the genreply-null fixture: presumably served-by confirmed (response.provider says DeepInfra?) but generation null. Then my check fires → exit 3 with named error `[generation] ...`. Good: N4(a).\n\nHmm, but what if the genreply-null fixture's response has no `provider` field either (so served_by empty → existing endpoint refusal)? Then N4 also passes (exit non-zero with named error). Either way exit non-zero. Good.\n\nBut wait: what if genreply-null fixture's call is a *direct* call (not through aggregator)? Then \"no /api/v1/generation reply\" wouldn't matter and the endpoint is the request host — evidenced by the request, not the reply. The fixture name suggests the generation reply is null. Hmm, for a direct provider call there's no /api/v1/generation at all. The bar says \"a model pass whose /api/v1/generation reply is missing/null\". Since or_call.py always calls OpenRouter, the fixture is an OpenRouter call. Fine.\n\nAdditionally, to be safe for N4(b)-style: also emit an x-plumbline flag naming the pass when generation is null? That would need to be in the record, but we refuse so nothing is written. N4 accepts (a). Good.\n\nAlso N6: the code path must have no fallback deriving endpoint from routing config or provider pins. But `endpoint_of` DOES return `pin.get(\"host\")` when served-by is confirmed fr