 custody SPEC section 4: \\`endpoint\\` is the host of the service that ANSWERED; \"a router that passed`\n15: `             the request on is not the service that answered\". A call made straight to a provider: the request`\n16: `             URL host. A call through an aggregator (AGGREGATORS, i.e. OpenRouter): the host the Orchestrator`\n17: `             DECLARED for that seat's upstream provider (custody_ledger.py pin, logged live before the seat's`\n18: `             first call), and only if every call's log shows that provider served it (response \\`provider\\`,`\n19: `             \\`openrouter_metadata\\`, or the logged /api/v1/generation stats). No pin, a pin declared after the`\n20: `             first call, no provider readable from the log, or any other provider serving or tried (a fallback):`\n21: `             refused. Nothing ever defaults to the model vendor's host, and no host is looked up from a table.`\n22: `  ROUTING    Each aggregator call's request body must ask for the pin: provider.order of exactly one slug (the`\n23: `             pinned one), provider.allow_fallbacks false, and provider.zdr true or provider.data_collection`\n24: `             \"deny\". What was asked is recorded; a request that did not ask for it is a hard GAP, not a refusal`\n25: `             (see docs/custody-emission.md, 'Routing request').`\n26: `  APPROVALS  An approval is a record of who approved, not a signature. It counts only from a party who is not`\n27: `             the orchestrating agent (inputs \\`orchestrator\\`) and not a model (the maker, an evaluator, or`\n28: `             \\`kind: \"model\"\\`). An agent may appear only when marked \\`kind: \"agent\"\\` WITH \\`adopted\\` set (a`\n29: `             published document it adopted). A name that reads like an agent but is not marked is refused.`\n30: `  REVISION   The emitter's own git commit and tree are recorded; a dirty tree (outside dot-plumbline/, which`\n31: `             is job state) or a mismatch