ng offsets (it tries line number then nearby). It's generally tolerant of wrong line numbers as long as the context is unique and counts are right. Counts must be right (no --recount).\n\nSo I must get the +/- counts exactly right; line numbers I should try to approximate but correctness of counts is key.\n\nLet me define hunks with counts carefully.\n\nHunk A old 7 lines:\n1 `        self.at = self.date or self.created                 # when the answer was given (server clock)`\n2 `        self.started = self.created or self.date            # when the generation began`\n3 `        self.at_basis = ...`\n4 `` (blank)\n5 `    @property`\n6 `    def label(self) -> str:`\n7 `        return f\"{self.log}#{self.name or self.i}\"`\n\nNew: 7 + 9 = 16.\n\nHeader: `@@ -85,7 +85,16 @@` (line numbers approximate). I need to pick plausible numbers. Base Call class around line 78-95. The `self.at` line maybe ~81. Let me estimate from the given file text: I can count roughly. Lines 1-... hard. I'll use numbers and rely on git apply's offset search. Risky but standard practice; git apply allows offsets. Yes — git apply will try to apply at the given line and search backwards/forwards. Fine.\n\nLet me count the base file to get better numbers. I'll count the custody_history.py content:\n\n1 \"\"\"What a custody record may say about its own history, and where each fact must come from.\n2 (blank)\n3 kit/emit_custody.py writes a custody/v1 record from a job's files. Iteration 1 trusted the Orchestrator's ledger\n4 (inputs/custody.json) for times, endpoints and approvers. Real runs showed what that costs: round-minute times\n5 typed after the fact (some later than the record itself), the model vendor's host recorded when every call went\n6 through a router to someone else, and the orchestrating agent recorded as the Specialist. A checker cannot see\n7 any of that, so the emitter must refuse to write it. This module holds those refusals. Stdlib only.\n8 (blank)\n9   TIMES      Every model call's time comes from its harness log: the HTTP `Date` response header (server-set),\n10             else the response's `created` epoch. A hand-entered time for the same event is kept only as a\n11             cross-check: if it differs from the log by more than TOLERANCE_S the emitter refuses. Event order\n12             (frozenAt < startedAt <= producedAt <= checks <= close; frozenAt <= approvals <= startedAt;\n13             disposition >= the last check) and \"nothing dated after the emit time\" are refusals too.\n14   ENDPOINTS  custody SPEC section 4: `endpoint` is the host of the service that ANSWERED; \"a router that passed\n15             the request on is not the service that answered\". A call made straight to a provider: the request\n16             URL host. A call through an aggregator (AGGREGATORS, i.e. OpenRouter): the host the Orchestrator\n17             DECLARED for that seat's upstream provider (custody_ledger.py pin, logged live before the seat's\n18             first call), and only if every call's log shows that provider served it (response `provider`,\n19             `openrouter_metadata`, or the logged /api/v1/generation stats). No pin, a pin declared after the\n20             first call, no provider readable from the log, or any other provider serving or tried (a fallback):\n21             refused. Nothing ever defaults to the model vendor's host, and no host is looked up from a table.\n22   ROUTING    Each aggregator call's request body must ask for the pin: provider.order of exactly one slug (the\n23             pinned one), provider.allow_fallbacks false, and provider.zdr true or provider.data_collection\n24             \"deny\". What was asked is recorded; a request that did not ask for it is a hard GAP, not a refusal\n25             (see docs/custody-emission.md, 'Routing request').\n26   APPROVALS  An approval is a record of who approved, not a signature. It counts only from a party who is not\n27             the orchestrating agent (inputs `orchestrator`) and not a model (the maker, an evaluator, or\n28             `kind: \"model\"`). An agent may appear only when marked `kind: \"agent\"` WITH `adopted` set (a\n29             published document it adopted). A name that reads like an agent but is not marked is refused.\n30   REVISION   The emitter's own git commit and tree are recorded; a dirty tree (outside dot-plumbline/, which\n31             is job state) or a mismatch with the stated revision (--expect-revision or\n32             $PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION, commit or tree, >= 7 hex) is refused.\n33 \"\"\"\n34 from __future__ import annotations\n35 import json, os, re, subprocess\n36 from datetime import datetime, timezone\n37 from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime\n38 from pathlib import Path\n39 from urllib.parse import urlparse\n40 (blank)\n41 # A hand-entered time may differ from the harness log by at most this many seconds. 60 s absorbs a time typed\n42 # to the minute (truncation loses up to 59 s) and ordinary clock skew between the harness host and the server;\n43 # it does not absorb a time typed from memory minutes later, which is the failure it exists to catch.\n44 TOLERANCE_S = 60\n45 (blank)\n46 # Request hosts that pass a request on to someone else. Their host is never a custody endpoint.\n47 AGGREGATORS = {\"openrouter.ai\": \"openrouter\", \"api.openrouter.ai\": \"openrouter\"}\n48 (blank)\n49 # Where an aggregator's log can say who served a call. All three are OpenRouter's documented fields:\n50 #   response `provider`              the provider display name on a sync response (openapi.json: \"the `provider`\n51 #                                    field of sync responses\"; not listed in ChatResult's schema)\n52 #   response `openrouter_metadata`   opt-in (request header X-OpenRouter-Metadata: enabled): endpoints.available[]\n53 #                                    {provider, selected} and attempts[] {provider, model, status}\n54 #   `generation` (harness-logged)   