ot be exactly aligned with `received_at`? Fine.\n\n### Source line numbers\n\nWe need ensure line numbers are correct. For JSON list pretty printed, `_line_of` searches for `\"sent_at\": \"...\"`. The harness log written by `or_call.py` uses `json.dumps(items, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\"`. It will have:\n```\n[\n  {\n    \"call\": \"...\",\n    ...\n    \"sent_at\": \"2026-...\",\n    ...\n```\nOur search finds. For `received_at`, finds. For `Date`, headers dict may be printed with `\"Date\": \"...\"`. Good. For JSONL, same.\n\nBut what if the log is a JSON list with multiple entries and same `sent_at` value? Sequential cursor for key ensures first occurrence for first call, second for second. But if the same value appears in another key before the next call's sent_at? We search exact key. Fine.\n\nFor `Date` header, if multiple headers with same Date value? Cursor. Fine.\n\nNeed `_line_of` handle `json.dumps(val)` for string with special chars. It will escape. The text uses same json.dumps. Good.\n\nNeed ensure `c.path` set. In `read_log`, after calls creation:\n```\n    for c in calls:\n        c.path = path\n```\nAlso set line numbers. We can add a function `_annotate_lines(text, calls)`.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nIn `Call.__init__`, add:\n```\n        self.path: Path | None = None\n        self.sent = parse_ts(raw.get(\"sent_at\"))\n        self.received = parse_ts(raw.get(\"received_at\"))\n        self.has_generation_key = \"generation\" in raw\n        self.generation = raw.get(\"generation\")\n        self.sent_line = self.received_line = self.date_line = self.created_line = None\n```\nNeed `parse_ts` defined before Call. Yes.\n\nIn `read_log`, after `calls = [...]`:\n```\n    for c in calls:\n        c.path = path\n    _annotate_lines(text, calls)\n```\nBut `text` is defined. For JSONL, `text` full. Good.\n\nDefine `_annotate_lines` before read_log or after. It uses `json`, `_hdr`. Let's place after `Call` class.\n\n```\ndef _line_of(text: str, needle: str, start: int = 0) -> tuple[int | None, int]:\n    i = text.find(needle, start)\n    if i < 0:\n        return None, start\n    return text.count(\"\\n\", 0, i) + 1, i + len(needle)\n\n\ndef _annotate_lines(text: str, calls: list[Call]) -> None:\n    cursors: dict[str, int] = {}\n    for c in calls:\n        raw = c.raw\n        for key, attr in ((\"sent_at\", \"sent_line\"), (\"received_at\", \"received_line\")):\n            val = raw.get(key)\n            if isinstance(val, str):\n                needle = f'\"{key}\": {json.dumps(val)}'\n                line, pos = _line_of(text, needle, cursors.get(key, 0))\n                if line:\n                    setattr(c, attr, line)\n                    cursors[key] = pos\n        hdrs = raw.get(\"response_headers\") or raw.get(\"headers\") or {}\n        d = _hdr(hdrs, \"Date\")\n        if isinstance(d, str):\n            for key in (\"Date\", \"date\"):\n                needle = f'\"{key}\": {json.dumps(d)}'\n                line, pos = _line_of(text, needle, cursors.get(\"Date\", 0))\n                if line:\n                    c.date_line = line\n                    cursors[\"Date\"] = pos\n                    break\n        created = (raw.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\")\n        if isinstance(created, (int, float)) and not isinstance(created, bool):\n            needle = f'\"created\": {json.dumps(created)}'\n            line, pos = _line_of(text, needle, cursors.get(\"created\", 0))\n            if line:\n                c.created_line = line\n                cursors[\"created\"] = pos\n```\n\nPotential issue: `json.dumps(created)` for float may output `1.0` while text has `1`? The harness response created is int epoch. Fine. For `Date` string, exact.\n\nIf `sent_at` is not RFC (e.g. invalid), `parse_ts` returns None. Then maker refuses. Good.\n\n### emit_custody.py source helper\n\nAdd after `rel_file` or near top:\n```\ndef src(job: Path, p: Path | None, line: int | None) -> dict | None:\n 