                "type": "reasoning.text",
                "text": "We need to produce a patch that fixes gaps 1-3 in the custody emitter for smoitozo/plumbline. We need understand the base code and what changes are needed. Let's parse requirements carefully.\n\nWe have base files: kit/emit_custody.py, kit/custody_history.py, kit/custody_ledger.py, tools/pack_stranger_bundle.py, docs/custody-emission.md, tests fixtures.\n\nGap 1 (N1-N3): maker.startedAt / producedAt must come from the logger's `sent_at` of the FIRST Maker call and `received_at` of the LAST Maker call (not the `created` epoch or `Date` header), each time with an `x-plumbline` source naming file and line.\n\nCurrently `custody_history.Call` has `at` = date or created (when answer given), `started` = created or date. `maker_history` uses `first.started` and `last.at`. Need to use `sent_at` and `received_at` from log entries, if present. The harness `or_call.py` writes `sent_at` and `received_at` on each entry. We need modify `Call` to capture `sent_at` and `received_at` from raw log, and use those for maker started/produced. Also need source naming file and line (x-plumbline source). The task says \"each time with an `x-plumbline` source naming file and line.\" Currently `x` in maker_history has `startedAt_basis` and `producedAt_basis` with label (log#call), but not line number. Need include line. Need inspect expected tests? The evaluator's S1 scripts compare to log. Need implement robustly.\n\nAlso N3: Every time field in record (`frozenAt`, `startedAt`, `producedAt`, `checks[].at`, approvals, disposition) has an `x-plumbline` source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time. Any time without a source fails. Currently many time fields lack explicit source. We need add source annotations for all time fields. Hmm this is broader. Need understand \"naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time.\" For harness-log derived times, we can name `file#call` or line. For ledger times, name `inputs/custody-events.jsonl` line N. For hand-entered? N3 says every time field has x-plumbline source naming ledger or harness-log file and line. If a time is hand-entered from custody.json? The task says \"Every time field in the record ... has an `x-plumbline` source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time. Any time without a source fails.\" So we need annotate. Existing code has some `x-plumbline` in approvals, checks, controls but not bar.frozenAt? disposition? startedAt/producedAt. Need add.\n\nGap 2 (N4-N6): when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply, never derive an `endpoint` from routing config or provider pins; refuse with a named error or record the CL1-15 evidence absent with an `x-plumbline` flag naming the pass.\n\nCurrently `endpoint_of` uses pin to derive endpoint if aggregator and pin and served. It uses `c.served` from response and generation. If generation is missing but response has `provider` or openrouter_metadata, it can still confirm served. But N4 says \"when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply, never derive an endpoint from routing config or provider pins; refuse with a named error or record the CL1-15 evidence absent with an x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\" So specifically for a model pass (evaluator? \"model pass\" likely each evaluator pass/call) whose `/api/v1/generation` reply is missing/null, we must not use pin-derived endpoint. We should either refuse (exit 3 named error) or record CL1-15 evidence absent with flag. The fixture `genreply-null` presumably has generation null. Need ensure emitter does not write endpoint from pin. For maker? \"model pass\" probably evaluator pass. But N4 criterion: \"On fixture genreply-null (a model pass whose /api/v1/generation reply is missing/null) the emitter either (a) exits non-zero with a named error, or (b) records that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE and writes an x-plumbline flag naming the pass. It never writes an endpoint no reply evidences.\" So if generation missing, no endpoint. Could choose refusal simpler? But N5 requires genreply-full passes. N6 checks no fallback deriving endpoint from routing config/pins. We can implement refusal when generation missing for a model call used for endpoint? But maker startedAt/producedAt can still be from sent/received. For evaluator endpoint, if no generation, can't confirm? Actually response `provider` might exist, but N4 specifically says no endpoint no reply evidences. \"reply\" likely generation reply. Could use response `provider`? The criterion says \"a model pass whose /api/v1/generation reply is missing/null\" and \"It never writes an endpoint no reply evidences.\" The generation reply evidences provider. If generation missing, no endpoint should be written. We could record absence flag and omit endpoint. But check.py must read NOT-CHECKABLE for CL1-15 evidence. The external entry for evaluator must have evidence absent? Let's think.\n\nCurrent `evaluator_history` returns endpoint = first.endpoint. If generation missing, endpoint_of may still use response provider or metadata. We need detect generation missing and avoid endpoint. Also external evidence: CL1-15 external holds harness log. If generation missing, maybe external evidence should be absent? The criterion (b): \"records that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE and writes an x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\" That suggests set external evidence null? But harness log is still evidence of configuration? CL1-15 requires harness log showing model and endpoint answered. If generation missing, harness log doesn't show endpoint? It may still show response provider. But requirement says generation reply missing -> CL1-15 evidence absent. We can add a gap/flag and set external evidence None? Need be careful not to break N7: bundle must carry every external[].evidence file; if None then not required? N7 says every `external[].evidence` file. If absent, maybe no file. But N7 also says every maker/evaluator harness log the record's times or endpoints came from. Even if endpoint absent, times come from log, so bundle must include harness log. So external evidence maybe still harness log? But \"CL1-15 evidence absent\" means the external entry's evidence should be absent? Let's examine check.py semantics? We don't have custody checker. But likely CL1-15 checks external[].evidence present and readable. If absent -> NOT-CHECKABLE. The task says \"records that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE and writes an x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\" So we need for that evaluator, external entry evidence = None (or omit) and x-plumbline flag naming pass. But N7 says bundle carries every `external[].evidence` file; if None, no file. However bundle also must carry every harness log times/endpoints came from. If times came from harness log, include it anyway. We can add harness log to bundle via new mechanism (N7) regardless.\n\nCould choose refusal (exit non-zero) for genreply-null. That may be simpler and allowed (a). But N7/N8 still need bundles for genreply-full and others. If we refuse genreply-null, evaluator may not require record? N4 allows exit non-zero. But N7 says \"Each stranger bundle zip contains...\" likely for all emitted records; genreply-null may not emit. The evaluator may run fixture genreply-null expecting either. A refusal might be acceptable. But N6 says no fallback deriving endpoint from routing config or provider pins. If we refuse on missing generation, no endpoint. Could implement named error. But also need make sure genreply-full works and endpoint equals reply's provider host. The generation reply's provider_name maps to pinned host? The endpoint is pin host if pin. \"each endpoint equals the reply's provider host\" — if generation reply says provider name, we need compare to pin provider and use pin host. That's existing.\n\nBut N2 uses maker-10min with two Maker calls; first sent_at and last received_at 600s apart. Need maker_history use sent_at first and received_at last. If log entries lack sent_at? The harness writes them. The base `Call` doesn't parse them. We'll add. For robustness fallback? N1 says \"read from Maker harness log ... A Maker call logged without both times fails.\" So if missing sent_at/received_at, should refuse or gap. We need ensure not fallback to created/date for maker times? Gap 1 says must come from sent_at/received_at (not created epoch or Date header). So if missing, fail (refuse or gap). We'll implement refusal via H.Refusals? maker_history currently uses ref. If missing sent_at/received_at, add refusal \"time\" maybe. But N1 expects source lines. We can add.\n\nGap 3 (N7-N8): stranger bundle must also carry `inputs/custody-events.jsonl` and every maker/evaluator harness log the record's times or endpoints came from, so a stranger can re-derive them.\n\nCurrently packer includes evidence files (checks/external) and context, but not custody-events.jsonl, and not harness logs unless external evidence. Need add:\n- `inputs/custody-events.jsonl` (maybe arc `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`).\n- Every maker/evaluator harness log referenced in record x-plumbline. Need identify from record. The record's x-plumbline has maker.x-plumbline.harness_log? Currently maker_history x has `harness_log` via route_x. Evaluator x too. Also `x-plumbline.events_log` has name. Need packer read record and include these. Need determine paths. Harness logs are in evidence_dir usually, named in x-plumbline. Could also be in job root? `find_log` checks ev_dir and job. The record x has `harness_log` only file name, not relative path. We can add to packer: for each x-plumbline harness_log (maker and evaluators), find under ev_dir or job. Also `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`. Also maybe `external[].evidence` already includes harness logs. But need ensure all. N7: \"plus inputs/custody-events.jsonl and every maker/evaluator harness log the record's times or endpoints came from.\" We can add entries under `inputs/` and `evidence/` maybe. Need N8 stranger re-derives startedAt, producedAt, each endpoint from bundled ledger/logs equal record. So bundle must have logs accessible; README maybe mention. The stranger will read record and logs. The exact in-zip path? N7 likely checks listing vs required list. We can choose paths: `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`, `evidence/<harness log>` (same as evidence dir). If harness log already in evidence via external, duplicate add is okay. If harness log in job root, we might put `evidence/<name>`. Need avoid collisions. We can create helper.\n\nAlso N8: stranger re-derives startedAt, producedAt and each endpoint from bundled ledger/logs, equal to record. This implies record's x-plumbline source must name file and line so stranger can read. The harness log is JSON list; line number in file? The log entries are pretty-printed JSON array, not JSONL. \"line\" maybe the line in the file where the entry starts? Or call label? N1 says \"x-plumbline source naming file and line.\" Hmm harness log is a JSON list pretty-printed; each entry spans multiple lines. But maybe they expect \"harness-maker.log#0\" or line number. N3 says \"that line carries that time.\" For JSON list, line number of entry? Could be line where `\"sent_at\"` appears. We need implement line numbers when reading log. `read_log` currently loses line. We can parse JSON and also compute line numbers for each entry? Or use JSONL if possible. The harness log is JSON list, so line number isn't straightforward. But we can find line of each entry by locating `\"sent_at\": \"<time>\"`? Hmm.\n\nLet's re-read N3: \"Every time field in the record (`frozenAt`, `startedAt`, `producedAt`, `checks[].at`, approvals, disposition) has an `x-plumbline` source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time. Any time without a source fails.\" This likely means each time field should have x-plumbline source like `{\"file\": \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\", \"line\": 2}` or `{\"file\": \"harness-maker.log\", \"line\": 10}`. The evaluator checks that line contains that time. For ledger events JSONL, easy. For harness log JSON list, they might accept line number of the `sent_at` or `received_at` line. We can compute line numbers by scanning raw text for each call's sent_at/received_at. But if duplicate times, need entry-specific. Alternatively, we can record source as `{\"file\": \"harness-maker.log\", \"line\": <line of entry>}`. How to find entry line? The JSON is pretty-printed list, entries start with `  {` maybe. Could compute by tracking positions during JSON parse? Python json doesn't provide line. We can write a simple scanner to find each entry's start line by counting `{` at top-level? Or simpler: for each call index, find the nth occurrence of `\"call\":` or `\"sent_at\"`. But N3 says line carries that time; if we point to line with `\"sent_at\": ...`, it carries time. For received_at similarly. For check time from `Date` header, line with `\"Date\": \"...\"` carries time. But for maker startedAt from sent_at, line with sent_at. For producedAt from received_at, line with received_at. For evaluator checks, current uses `call.at` (Date header or created). But N3 requires checks[].at source naming file and line. Could use `Date` header line. Or maybe use received_at? The task only changes maker times to sent_at/received_at, not checks. But N3 all time fields need source. Existing checks x has `call` and `at_basis` but no line. We can add `at_source` with file/line. Similarly controls.\n\nWe need be careful not to over-engineer beyond tests. But evaluator S1 likely checks `x-plumbline` keys. We need infer expected key names? The task doesn't specify exact schema, just \"has an `x-plumbline` source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line\". Could be any key. We should choose consistent e.g. `\"time_source\": {\"file\": ..., \"line\": ...}` or `\"at_source\"`. Existing code has `time_source` for approvals (string). We can augment.\n\nLet's inspect existing tests/fixtures to understand shape. We only have base snippets, not full fixtures. But we can reason.\n\nWe need produce patch against base. We can modify files. Need ensure tests/validate_schemas.py still passes. We don't have its content, but likely checks emitter outputs for fixtures. Changes must not break. We can run? No environment. Need be conservative.\n\nLet's map changes:\n\n1. `kit/custody_history.py`:\n   - `Call` should capture `sent_at`, `received_at` from raw (and maybe line numbers). Add attributes `sent`, `received`, and line numbers? For source.\n   - Need parse times with RFC regex; harness writes `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S.%fZ` which matches RFC? Current RFC `^\\d{4}-...(\\\\.\\d+)?Z$` yes.\n   - `sent` = parse_ts(raw.get(\"sent_at\")) or None. `received` = parse_ts(raw.get(\"received_at\")) or None.\n   - Maybe `sent_at_basis`.\n   - For line numbers, `read_log` can annotate each Call with `entry_line` and maybe `sent_line`, `received_line`, `date_line`. How? We can implement a helper to find line numbers in raw text. Simpler: `read_log` currently parses whole file. We can also read text. For JSON list, we can find each call's `\"sent_at\"` value line by searching for the exact JSON string `json.dumps(value)`? But pretty-printed with `\"sent_at\": \"...\"`. We can search for `\"sent_at\": \"value\"`. If multiple same value, ambiguous. But we can assign in order of occurrences: for each call index, find next occurrence after previous. Similarly for `\"received_at\"`, `\"Date\"`, `\"created\"`. We can write `line_of(text, key, value, start=0)`. But the log may be JSON Lines; then each line is entry. We can detect. Let's implement in `read_log`:\n      - text read.\n      - Determine if JSON list/dict or JSONL.\n      - For each item, create Call with `line` maybe line number of item start. For JSON list, we can compute entry start by searching for `\"call\":`? But call may absent. Maybe use the line of the first key in entry? We can find line of `\"sent_at\"` for sent source, etc.\n   - But do we need line in `Call`? We can store `sent_line`, `received_line`, `date_line`, `created_line`. `Call` can have `src` dict.\n   - Since `Call` is created from raw and we have text, we can pass line hints to constructor. Modify `read_log` to compute for each item. Simpler: after creating Call, scan text for its `sent_at` and `received_at` string values sequentially. But multiple calls may have same times? Unlikely. We can maintain search offsets.\n   - We need source for check `at` (Date header). `c.date` line. We can find line of `\"Date\": \"<raw>\"`. But header keys may be lowercase? `_hdr` handles case. We can store raw Date value. Find `\"Date\"` case-insensitive? We can search for the value string. Simpler: for source line, use entry start line, which \"carries\" the time somewhere in entry. N3 says \"that line carries that time\" — if entry spans multiple lines, entry start line may not carry time. So better point to line with the time value. For sent_at, line with `\"sent_at\": ...`. For received_at, line with `\"received_at\": ...`. For Date, line with `\"Date\": ...`. For created, line with `\"created\": ...`.\n   - We can implement `find_line(text, needle, start=0)` returning 1-based line number. For each call, we know exact JSON string representation? The log is pretty printed with `json.dumps(..., indent=2)`, so `\"sent_at\": \"2026-...\"` with space after colon. We can search for `f'\"{key}\": {json.dumps(value)}'`. But for headers, key might be \"Date\" and value. We can search `f'\"{key}\": {json.dumps(value)}'` maybe with case variants. Or just search for `json.dumps(value)` first occurrence? Could be ambiguous. We can search for `\"sent_at\":` then the value. Let's implement robust enough.\n\n   - However, maybe N3 only expects source for fields derived from ledger (frozenAt, approvals, disposition) and harness log (startedAt, producedAt, checks). Existing code already has `x-plumbline` on checks with `call` and `at_basis`; we can add `at_source` line. Controls similarly. Approvals have `time_source` but not file/line; add `source` with file/line for live event or hand-entered? N3 says source naming ledger or harness-log file and line. For approvals, if live, source is events log line. If hand-entered and no live, maybe no source -> fail. But fixture job-rejected probably has no custody-events? Wait job-rejected fixture likely includes inputs/custody-events.jsonl? The task says fixtures have `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`, harness logs and `inputs/custody.json`. job-rejected may have events? The base emitter reads events. If no events, approvals are hand-entered. N3 would fail. But maybe evaluator only runs held-out fixtures with events. Still, existing tests may have job-rejected without events. We must not break tests. We can add source only when available; if not, maybe a gap? But N3 says any time without source fails. For fixtures used by S1, they likely have events. For job-rejected, tests may not check N3. We can add source for hand-entered as `{\"file\": \"inputs/custody.json\", \"line\": ...}`? But N3 says ledger or harness-log file. custody.json is ledger. We can compute line number of the time value in custody.json. That satisfies \"ledger\". Good. So for any hand-entered time from custody.json, we can find its line. For approval `at`, if live, source events log line; else custody.json line. For frozenAt, if live freeze event, source events log line; else custody.json line. For disposition, if live, events log; else if fallback emission time, source? N3 says disposition time must have source. If emission time, source is emitter? Not ledger/harness. Could be a problem. But maybe fixtures always have disposition. The base code allows fallback emission time. N3 says any time without source fails. We could record source as `{\"file\": \"kit/emit_custody.py\", \"line\": ...}`? Not ledger/harness. Better ensure disposition has source: if no recorded disposition time, maybe use events log or custody.json. If neither, it's emission time; we could add a gap/refusal? But existing behavior may be used. For S1, disposition likely logged. We'll handle.\n\n   - `startedAt`/`producedAt` source from harness log line. Add `x-plumbline` at predicate level? Currently maker.x-plumbline has `startedAt_basis`. N3 says time field has x-plumbline source. Could put `predicate.x-plumbline.startedAt_source = {file,line}` and `producedAt_source`. Or under maker.x-plumbline? The criterion says every time field in record has an x-plumbline source. It may inspect `x-plumbline` near field? For startedAt/producedAt, they are top-level predicate fields. We can add to `pred[\"x-plumbline\"][\"times\"]` or new keys. Existing `xp[\"times\"]` has startedAt/producedAt strings. We can change to include source. But to avoid breaking, add `xp[\"startedAt_source\"]`, `xp[\"producedAt_source\"]`, etc. Also maker.x-plumbline could have. We'll add both? Need not overdo.\n\n   - For checks, `c[\"x-plumbline\"]` already exists. Add `at_source` = {file, line} or `time_source`. For controls, add `at_source`. For approvals, add `at_source` under approval x-plumbline. For bar.frozenAt, bar has no x-plumbline? We can add `bar[\"x-plumbline\"] = {\"frozenAt_source\": {...}}`. For disposition, `disposition[\"x-plumbline\"] = {\"at_source\": {...}}`.\n\n   - Need source naming file and line. We can create helper `source_of(file, line)` returning `{\"file\": rel, \"line\": n}`. For harness log, file name maybe `harness-maker.log` (relative to evidence dir? The record x has `harness_log` name). The evaluator likely knows evidence_dir. N7 bundle will have `evidence/<name>`. We can name `evidence/<name>`? Existing `x` route_x uses `log.name` only. N1 says source naming file and line. Maybe file should be `harness-maker.log` and line. The record's x-plumbline has `harness_log` maybe. We can include full relative path? `find_log` returns Path; we can compute relative to job. Better use `str(log.relative_to(job))` e.g. `custody/evidence/harness-maker.log`. That is unambiguous. But existing route_x uses `log.name`. Changing to relative may break existing tests? They may expect name only? The docs say `harness_log` name. The base code `route_x(log, calls)` sets `x = {\"harness_log\": log.name, ...}`. To be safe, keep `harness_log` as name, add `harness_log_path` maybe. For source, use relative path. The task says naming file and line; relative path is fine. We'll add `source` with `file` = relative path (POSIX), `line`.\n\n   - Need compute line numbers for harness logs. We can extend `Call` with `src` dict. Let's design:\n     - In `read_log`, after parsing items, we have text. For each item index, create Call. Then compute line numbers for relevant keys using a cursor. Since JSON list pretty-printed, occurrences appear in order. We can search for key/value pairs in text starting from cursor. For each call:\n        - `sent_at` value: if present, find line of `\"sent_at\": \"<value>\"` after cursor; set c.sent_line.\n        - `received_at`: similarly.\n        - `Date` header: raw.get(\"response_headers\") or headers; find key \"Date\" value. Search `\"Date\": \"<value>\"` or case-insensitive. But JSON may have other Date? Use cursor.\n        - `created`: search `\"created\": <number>`.\n       But cursor should advance to after the entry to avoid finding same value in next call? We can find all occurrences in order; since we process calls in order, search from previous line. But if same value repeated, sequential search works.\n     - However, if log is JSONL, each line is an entry; line numbers easy: enumerate lines. But text parse via json fails for JSONL? Current code first tries json.loads(text); if ValueError then JSONL. We can handle both.\n     - For JSONL, we can set `c.line = n` for each non-empty line. And `sent_line = n`, `received_line = n`, etc. But N3 \"that line carries that time\" — the whole entry line carries all times. Good.\n     - For JSON list, find specific key lines. If not found, fallback to entry start line? Need entry start. We can find line of first `{` for each entry. Hard. But we can just search for key. If not found, maybe line 0.\n     - We can also add `c.line` as the line of `\"call\"` or entry start. But not needed if we have specific lines.\n\n   - `Call` needs `sent`, `received`. Add in __init__:\n     ```\n     self.sent = parse_ts(raw.get(\"sent_at\"))\n     self.received = parse_ts(raw.get(\"received_at\"))\n     ```\n     But `parse_ts` defined above? Yes. `Call` class defined after parse_ts. Good.\n     Also maybe `sent_raw`, `received_raw`.\n     `self.at` currently date or created. Keep for evaluator checks. But for maker, use sent/received.\n\n   - Add attributes for line numbers: `self.sent_line = None`, etc. We can set after construction.\n\n2. `kit/emit_custody.py`:\n   - `maker_history`: use first.sent and last.received. Need choose first by sent time, last by received time? Gap 1: \"sent_at of the FIRST Maker call and received_at of the LAST Maker call\". First/last likely chronological by call order? The harness log list order is call order. But \"first\" and \"last\" could be by sent/received. For maker-10min two calls, first call sent_at and last call received_at. We should sort by sent for first, by received for last? If calls overlap? Use min by sent and max by received. But if a call lacks sent/received, fail. We'll filter? Current `calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]` for maker. Need change to require sent/received for maker. The maker calls may have at but not sent/received (older logs). N1 says a Maker call logged without both times fails. So we should check all maker calls have sent and received? \"A Maker call logged without both times fails.\" Likely if first lacks sent or last lacks received. But to be safe, require every maker call has both? Maybe only first and last. But N1 says \"A Maker call logged without both times fails.\" Could mean any Maker call. We'll check all calls have sent and received; if any missing, add refusal. But if extra call missing? The harness writes all. Fine.\n   - In `maker_history`:\n     ```\n     calls = [c for c in calls if c.at or c.sent or c.received]? \n     ```\n     We need still annotate endpoints. Keep calls with at? If a call has sent/received but no Date/created, endpoint still can be read. `annotate` doesn't need at. Current code filters `calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]`, which would drop calls lacking Date/created. For maker, we need sent/received; maybe keep if sent and received. But endpoint can be read. Let's change: `calls = [c for c in calls if c.sent and c.received]`? But if some calls missing, we want refusal. Better:\n        - `all_calls = H.read_log(...)`\n        - filter by m.get(\"calls\") if provided.\n        - For each call, if not (c.sent and c.received): ref.add(\"time\", f\"maker: {c.label} carries no sent_at/received_at; the maker's startedAt/producedAt must come from the harness log's send/receive times\") \n        - If no calls, return {}.\n        - Use calls with sent/received for first/last. If any missing, we can still proceed? But ref will cause exit 3. Good.\n     - `started = first.sent`, `produced = last.received`.\n     - Cross-check hand-entered ci startedAt/producedAt against these.\n     - `x`: add `startedAt_basis` = f\"{first.label} sent_at\", `producedAt_basis` = f\"{last.label} received_at\". Add `startedAt_source` and `producedAt_source` with file/line. Need get line numbers from Call. We'll set `first.sent_line`, `last.received_line`.\n     - `route_x` currently uses `log.name`. Keep. Add maybe `harness_log_path`.\n     - `endpoint`: last.endpoint. But for maker endpoint, if generation missing? N4 is about model pass; maker? It says \"when a model pass has no /api/v1/generation reply, never derive an endpoint from routing config or provider pins\". Maker is a model call but not a \"pass\"? Maybe also applies. To be safe, apply to all model calls (maker and evaluator): if generation missing, do not derive endpoint from pin. But maker endpoint may be required. If missing, we can omit endpoint and add gap/flag? For N4 specifically evaluator pass. But N5 genreply-full all replies present. For genreply-null, if maker also missing, maybe refuse. We can implement a general check in `endpoint_of` or `annotate`: if `c.generation` is None (or raw generation null) and c.host in AGGREGATORS, do not use pin; add refusal \"endpoint\" named. But N4 allows refusal. However, if we refuse for any missing generation, genreply-null will exit 3. That's acceptable (a). But N4 also says \"or record ...\". Refusal is allowed. But N7/N8 might still need bundle for genreply-null? N7 says each stranger bundle zip; if no record emitted, maybe not. The evaluator may not require bundle for refused fixture. But N9 control uses bundle with ledger removed; likely on a successful record. So refusal for genreply-null is okay. But need ensure not over-refuse when generation missing but response provider present? N4 says never derive endpoint from routing config or provider pins. It could still use response provider? \"It never writes an endpoint no reply evidences.\" The \"reply\" likely generation reply. So if generation missing, no endpoint. Refuse is safest.\n     - But if we refuse on missing generation for any call, then `genreply-null` exits 3. The criterion N4 (a) passes. Need named error. We can add ref row `endpoint` or `generation` with pass name. Good.\n     - However, we must not break `genreply-full` (all replies present). Fine.\n     - What about maker calls where generation missing but response provider present? The fixture `genreply-null` says \"a model pass whose /api/v1/generation reply is missing/null\" — maybe only evaluator pass. If we refuse any missing generation, okay.\n     