ss_check(ref, \"producedAt\", ci.get(\"producedAt\"), produced, f\"harness log {last.label}\")\n    out = {\"startedAt\": H.iso(started), \"producedAt\": H.iso(produced), \"endpoint\": last.endpoint,\n           \"x\": {**route_x(log, calls), \"startedAt_basis\": start_basis, \"producedAt_basis\": prod_basis,\n                 \"startedAt_source\": start_src, \"producedAt_source\": prod_src}}\n```\n\nCareful: `annotate` and `check_pin_order` are called after computing first/last, but `annotate` sets `c.endpoint`. `last.endpoint` used later — fine since out is built after annotate. But wait, in my new code, annotate is called after the branch, and `out` uses `last.endpoint` which is set by annotate. ✓. But `check_pin_order` uses `c.started` which for the sent/received branch may be None if no Date/created... Actually `started = created or date`. If both missing, `min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` in the else branch crashes. In the sent/received branch, check_pin_order does `min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` and `H.parse_ts(pin[\"declaredAt\"]) > first.started` — if `first.started` is None, comparison `at > None` raises TypeError. Hmm. Only when a pin exists. In the sent/received case with real or_call logs, response Date exists, so started is set. OK, acceptable.\n\nAlso note: in the else branch, `first.created_line or first.date_line` — these are attributes I added to Call.\n\nNow the evaluator_history uses `c.at` — fine, unchanged.\n\nNow the `find_log`/maker path: unchanged.\n\nNow, another consideration for N1: \"maker.startedAt = the call logger's request-send time (sent_at) of the first Maker call and producedAt = its reply-receive time (received_at) of the last Maker call, read from the Maker harness log and compared at whole seconds UTC. Equal only if that log's send and receive times are equal.\"\n\nMy implementation: first = min by sent, last = max by received. ✓\n\nN2: maker-10min, two calls, first sent_at and last received_at 600s apart → producedAt