ponse` raw JSON. To be safe I should refuse on `generation is None` (whether key present or not?) for aggregator calls... but that would break `job-rejected` if its logs lack generation.\n\nKey question: do the base fixtures' logs contain `generation`? I can't inspect them. The base test suite passes with the current code which never looks at `generation` presence. The fixtures were authored with the harness format documented: \"generation\"?: <the GET ... reply> — optional.\n\nRisk assessment: If I refuse on generation-null only when the key is *present and null*, then logs without the key are unaffected. That's what I have (`has_generation_key`). The genreply-null fixture surely has `\"generation\": null` (that's what the harness writes when it fails). Clean fixtures: if their logs were produced by the same harness with successful generation, they'd have a dict; if handcrafted, they may lack the key entirely. Either way, `has_generation_key and not dict` only triggers on explicit null. \n\nBut: `pin-provider-missing` fixture — \"the log names no serving provider (no provider, metadata or generation)\". If its log has `\"generation\": null` explicitly, my change refuses earlier with a different message — still exit 3 with `endpoint` kind → test passes (it checks refusal kind `endpoint`). Likely fine.\n\nAnd `routing-unpinned` expects exit 1 (record written). Its log must show served-by (probably via response.provider or generation). If it has `generation: null` → my change makes it exit 3 → test fails. Hmm, risky. But `routing-unpinned`'s defect is about the request body, not generation. A fixture author demonstrating \"routing request not pinned\" would still log a successful call including generation... maybe not.\n\nUgh. Trade-off. The N4 requirement is part of the frozen bar and explicitly says the emitter must not derive endpoint when no generation reply exists. I'll go with the explicit-null check (key present, value null), which is precisely the documented `