ine-by-line works): line number = line index.\n- If JSON pretty list: find entry start lines by `^  \\{$`.\n- If JSON compact (single line or multiple): hmm.\n\nActually, maybe a cleaner approach: for each entry, find the line number of a specific key's value. E.g., for `sent_at` value V, search lines for a line containing `\"sent_at\"` and V. Since V is unique-ish (microsecond timestamp), this works. For `received_at`, same. For the entry's general position, not needed.\n\nBut if two entries have identical sent_at (unlikely with microseconds), ambiguity. Could scope by entry order: find all lines containing `\"sent_at\"`, they appear in entry order, so the i-th occurrence corresponds to entry i. That's robust! Because each entry has exactly one `sent_at` line (if written by or_call). Similarly `received_at`.\n\nBut what if the log format doesn't have sent_at/received_at (older fixtures)? Then we fall back to Date/created and need line for those. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what's actually needed. The evaluator's fixtures (`single-call`, `maker-10min`, `genreply-null`, `genreply-full`) are written by the harness described, which writes `sent_at`/`received_at`. So for those, sent_at/received_at exist. The existing test suite fixtures (job-rejected, failures) — we need them to still pass. If those logs don't have sent_at/received_at, then the new code would fail to find startedAt/producedAt. But the base code used Date/created. Hmm.\n\nWait, but the task says the existing test suite must still pass. If job-rejected's harness log lacks sent_at/received_at, and we now require them, then job-rejected emission might fail (gaps/refusals) and break tests. So I need to know job-rejected's log format.\n\nWe don't have the fixture files. But the base code's `maker_history` uses `first.started` and `last.at` from Date/created. The tests presumably assert the record has certain values. If I switch to sent_at/received_at and the fixture lacks them, I break tests.\n\nHmm. Let me th