isfies N1 for logs with them.\n\nWait, but N1 requires startedAt = sent_at of first call, producedAt = received_at of last call. If I fall back, a log without them would use Date/created, but N1's evaluator would fail if it expected sent_at. But the held-out fixtures have them, so they use sent_at. Good. And existing test fixtures without them still work via fallback. That's a safe compromise.\n\nBut hold on: the \"single-call\" fixture has \"one Maker call, send ≠ receive\". If we fall back to Date/created, startedAt would be created and producedAt would be Date, which are server times, and send ≠ receive wouldn't be reflected. N1 says startedAt = sent_at, producedAt = received_at. With one call, sent_at ≠ received_at, so startedAt ≠ producedAt. If we used created/Date, they might be equal or ordered differently. So the evaluator specifically checks that startedAt ≠ producedAt when send ≠ receive. Our code with sent_at/received_at gives that. Good.\n\nSo: use sent_at/received_at when present. For maker:\n- startedAt = first call's sent_at\n- producedAt = last call's received_at\n\n\"first\" and \"last\" — by what ordering? The bar N2: \"two Maker calls; the first call's sent_at and the last call's received_at 600 s apart\". So first call = earliest (by sent_at or log order?), last call = latest. The base code uses `min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` and `max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)`. I should use sent_at for ordering first, received_at for ordering last. Or log order? The harness appends in call order, so log order is chronological. But to be safe, use min sent_at for first, max received_at for last. Actually \"the first Maker call\" likely means the first in time. Let me use min by sent_at for first, max by received_at for last. Hmm, but if calls overlap? Unlikely.\n\nActually, simpler and matches base: first = min by started (where started = sent_at if available else created/date), last = max by at (where at = received_at if available else date/created). Let