line is expected. Let me add both: a top-level `sources` map and per-object source. Actually, adding per-check source is easy. For approvals, add `x-plumbline.time_source` currently is \"hand-entered\"/\"logged live\". I can change/add `x-plumbline.at_source` = \"file:line\". For disposition, add to `x-plumbline` something.\n\nHmm, let me be systematic. Let me create a helper that computes line numbers for ledger events and harness log fields.\n\nLet me design the ledger event line tracking. `events_log` currently reads the file and returns latest per key. I'll augment it to also store line numbers:\n- For each event, record the line number.\n- `out[\"_lines\"]`? Or embed in each event `e[\"_line\"] = n`.\n\nLet me add `e[\"_line\"] = n` when parsing. Then:\n- freeze event: `ev_log[\"freeze\"][\"_line\"]`\n- approve events: `ev_log[\"approve\"][role][\"_line\"]`\n- dispose: `ev_log[\"dispose\"][\"_line\"]`\n- pin: `ev_log[\"pin\"][seat][\"_line\"]`\n\nAnd `ev_log[\"_file\"][\"name\"]` = \"inputs/custody-events.jsonl\".\n\nFor harness logs, I need line numbers of `sent_at` and `received_at` for each call. Let me augment `read_log` to attach line info. But `read_log` is in custody_history.py. I'll modify `Call` to store `self.lines = {}` mapping field -> line. And `read_log` computes them.\n\nHow to compute line numbers robustly:\n- Read text and lines.\n- If JSONL (line-by-line parse works): each call's line = the line number of the JSON line. Also `sent_at`/`received_at` are on that same line (compact). So line = that line.\n- If JSON list/object pretty-printed: find the line containing `\"sent_at\": \"<value>\"`. Use occurrence order.\n\nLet me implement a function `_field_lines(lines, items, key)`:\n```python\ndef _field_line(lines, value, key, occurrence):\n    pat = f'\"{key}\"'\n    count = 0\n    for i, ln in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        if pat in ln and str(value) in ln:\n            if count == occurrence:\n                return i\n            count += 1\n    return None\n```\n\nHmm, occurrence scoping. Actually simpler: for each call, search all lines for a line containing both the key and the exact serialized value. Since timestamps are unique with microseconds, this is unambiguous. If not unique, pick the occurrence matching the call index among matches. Let me do:\n```python\nmatches = [i for i, ln in enumerate(lines, 1) if f'\"{key}\"' in ln and f'\"{value}\"' in ln]\n```\nThen for call index j, use matches[j] if len(matches)==len(calls) else matches[0]. This works for pretty JSON where each call has one sent_at line.\n\nBut if the whole JSON is compact (one line), all matches are line 1. Then line=1 for all, which still \"carries that time\" (the line contains the time). That's fine for N3! The line carries the time. Even if all fields point to line 1, the line contains the value. So compact JSON still satisfies \"that line carries that time\". Good.\n\nSo a robust approach: find any line containing the exact value string. If multiple, use the call's ordinal among matching lines. Let me implement per-call by searching for the value.\n\nActually, even simpler: I'll store for each call a function or the raw text lines, and compute source at usage time. But `Call` is created in `read_log` which has the text. Let me pass `lines` to `Call` and store. Then `Call.source(key)` returns \"log:line\".\n\nLet me define:\n```python\nclass Call:\n    def __init__(self, log, i, raw, lines=None, index=None):\n        ...\n        self.lines = lines or []\n        self.index = i\n        self.sent = parse_ts(raw.get(\"sent_at\")) if raw.get(\"sent_at\") else None\n        self.received = parse_ts(raw.get(\"received_at\")) if raw.get(\"received_at\") else None\n        # keep started/at but prefer sent/received for ordering\n        ...\n    def line_of(self, key, value):\n        # find a line containing the value\n        if not value: return None\n        vs = str(value)\n        hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(self.lines, 1) if vs in ln]\n        if not hits:\n            return None\n        return hits[min(self.index, len(hits)-1)]\n```\n\nWait, `self.index` is the call index i, but `hits` could include lines from other entries if the value repeats. Timestamps unique, so hits likely one. For Date header (not unique format), might repeat if two calls same second. But we mostly use sent_at/received_at.\n\nHmm, but `line_of` with index mapping assumes hits are in entry order and there's one hit per entry. If the value appears in multiple fields of the same entry (e.g., request body has a timestamp equal to sent_at — unlikely), then hits > entries. Let me instead scope by searching only the exact `\"sent_at\": \"value\"` pattern. That's unique per entry (each entry has one sent_at). Then hits length = number of entries that have that exact sent_at. For unique timestamps, hits = [line of this entry]. Good.\n\nLet me implement:\n```python\ndef line_of(self, key, value):\n    if value is None: return None\n    pat = f'\"{key}\": \"{value}\"'\n    hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(self.lines, 1) if pat in ln]\n    if not hits:\n        # try without quotes / whitespace variation\n        hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(self.lines, 1) if f'\"{key}\"' in ln and str(value) in ln]\n    if not hits: return None\n    return hits[min(self.index, len(hits)-1)]\n```\n\nBut `value` should be the raw string from JSON (e.g., \"2026-10-27T21:53:35.123456Z\"). Good.\n\nNow, `H.iso(d)` truncates to seconds. For source we use raw `raw.get(\"sent_at\")`.\n\nLet me now plan the code changes file by file.\n\n### kit/custody_history.py\n\nChanges:\n1. `Call.__init__`: accept `lines` (list of text lines) and `index`. Parse `sent_at`/`received_at`. Keep `started`/`at` but define:\n   - `self.sent = parse_ts(raw.get(\"sent_at\"))`\n   - `self.received = parse_ts(raw.get(\"received_at\"))`\n   - `self.started = self.sent or self.created or self.date`\n   - `self.at = self.received or self.date or self.created`\n  