ry`:\n```python\nfirst = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\nlast = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\nstarted, produced = first.started, last.at\n```\nWith `started = sent or created/date` and `at = received or date/created`. For first call, sent_at. For last, received_at. Good.\n\nThen:\n```python\nout = {\"startedAt\": H.iso(started), \"producedAt\": H.iso(produced), ...}\n```\n\nAdd sources:\n```python\nout[\"x\"][\"startedAt_source\"] = first.source_of(\"sent_at\") or first.source_of(\"created\") ...\n```\n\nLet me define `Call.source_of(key)` returning \"log:line\" using the raw value of that key. For sent_at, raw value is `self.raw.get(\"sent_at\")`.\n\n```python\ndef source_of(self, key):\n    val = self.raw.get(key)\n    if val is None: return None\n    n = self.line_of(key, val)\n    return f\"{self.log}:{n}\" if n else f\"{self.log}:?\"\n```\n\nIf line not found, maybe just file name? N3 requires line. For JSONL/compact, line found (1 or actual). For pretty, found. OK.\n\nNow for startedAt, if sent_at present, source_of(\"sent_at\"). Else source_of(\"created\") or source_of(\"Date\")? Date is in headers, not raw top-level. Hmm. For fallback, the source would be the Date header line. `self.raw.get(\"response_headers\")` contains Date. I can find the line containing the Date value. Let me add:\n```python\ndef source_of_time(self):\n    # the field that carried self.at\n    if self.received: return self.source_of(\"received_at\")\n    d = _hdr(self.raw.get(\"response_headers\") or self.raw.get(\"headers\") or {}, \"Date\")\n    if d: return self.source_of_value(\"Date\", d)\n    if self.created: return self.source_of(\"created\")  # created is in response, not top-level\n```\n\nWait, `created` is `resp.get(\"created\")`, i.e., `raw[\"response\"][\"created\"]`. The line containing it would be `\"created\": 1234567890`. `self.raw.get(\"created\")` is None. So source_of needs to look into response. Let me generalize: search lines for the value string regardless of key? That could match multiple. Better to pass the actual value and search for it.\n\nLet me define:\n```python\ndef source_of_value(self, value, key=None):\n    if value is None: return None\n    vs = str(value)\n    hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(self.lines, 1) if vs in ln and (key is None or f'\"{key}\"' in ln)]\n    if not hits:\n        hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(self.lines, 1) if vs in ln]\n    if not hits: return None\n    return f\"{self.log}:{hits[min(self.index, len(hits)-1)]}\"\n```\n\nThen:\n- sent source: `source_of_value(raw.get(\"sent_at\"), \"sent_at\")`\n- received source: `source_of_value(raw.get(\"received_at\"), \"received_at\")`\n- created source: `source_of_value(resp.get(\"created\"), \"created\")`\n- Date source: `source_of_value(date_header_value, \"Date\")`\n\nBut `self.index` is the call index among calls, not among hits. If value unique, fine. If created timestamps repeat (same second), multiple hits; using index might pick wrong line. But lines all contain the same value anyway (if same), so any line \"carries that time\". Actually if two entries have the same created value, both lines carry it, so picking either is fine for \"that line carries that time\". Good.\n\nHmm, but `self.index` might exceed hits-1 if hits fewer than calls (e.g., only one entry has that value). Then `min` picks last. Fine.\n\nLet me simplify and not overthink. For the main path (sent_at/received_at), values are unique with microseconds.\n\n4. `evaluator_history`: similarly, the returned `calls` mapping. The `link_call` uses `call.at`. With `at` = received_at, checks[].at = received_at. Source = received_at line.\n\nIn `build`, for checks:\n```python\nif call is not None:\n    ...\n    at_v, xc = H.iso(call.at), {\"call\": call.label, \"at_basis\": call.at_basis}\n```\nAdd source:\n```python\nxc[\"at_source\"] = call.source_of_at()\n```\nwhere `source_of_at` returns source of received_at or Date or created.\n\nLet me add a method `source_at()`:\n```python\ndef source_at(self):\n    if self.received: return self.source_of_value(self.raw.get(\"received_at\"), \"received_at\")\n    d = _hdr(self.raw.get(\"response_headers\") or self.raw.get(\"headers\") or {}, \"Date\")\n    if d: return self.source_of_value(d, \"Date\")\n    return self.source_of_value((self.raw.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\"), \"created\")\n```\n\nAnd `source_started()`:\n```python\ndef source_started(self):\n    if self.sent: return self.source_of_value(self.raw.get(\"sent_at\"), \"sent_at\")\n    return self.source_of_value((self.raw.get(\"response\") or {}).get(\"created\"), \"created\") or \\\n           self.source_of_value(_hdr(...Date...), \"Date\")\n```\n\nWait, started = sent or created or date. So source accordingly.\n\n5. For approvals and freeze and disposition, add line sources from `events_log`.\n\nLet me modify `events_log` to add `_line` to each event:\n```python\nfor n, ln in enumerate(...):\n    ...\n    e[\"_line\"] = n\n    if e.get(\"event\") == \"approve\":\n        out[\"approve\"][e.get(\"role\")] = e\n    ...\n```\n\nThen in `build`, for each approval:\n```python\nif live:\n    xa[\"at_source\"] = f\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:{live['_line']}\"\n```\nBut only if the approval's `at` equals the logged one (cross-checked). If `live` exists, use its line. If not, the approval's at came from custody.json (hand-entered) — no ledger line. Then N3 fails? The bar says \"Any time without a source fails.\" So we need a ledger line. If the fixture's custody.json has approvals not in events log, that's a problem. But fixtures have custody-events.jsonl presumably with approvals. Let me assume.\n\nActually, to be safe, if no live event, we could still point to custody.json line? But N3 says \"a ledger or harness-log file and line\". custody.json is a ledger? It says \"ledger\" could mean custody.json or custody-events.jsonl. Hmm. The task says \"naming a ledger or harness-log file and line\". The `inputs/custody.json` is the Orchestrator's ledger. So pointing to custody.json line might count. But \"that line carries that time\" — the custody.json line `\"at\": \"...\"` carries it. So we could use custody.json line as fallback.\n\nBut the bar N3 specifically: \"has an x-plumbline source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line\". The custody.json is described as \"the Orchestrator's ledger\" in the emitter docstring. So yes, custody.json qualifies as a ledger.\n\nHmm, but the live event log is also a ledger. Let me provide sources from whichever file the value came from:\n- If live event matches, source = custody-events.jsonl:line.\n- Else, source = custody.json:line (find the line in custody.json containing the value).\n\nFinding a line in custody.json for a specific approval's `at` is tricky (multiple ats). Could search for the exact value string; might match multiple approvals if same time. But \"that line carries that time\" — any matching line works. Let me implement a generic `line_of_value(path, value)` helper.\n\nActually, let me add a helper in emit_custody.py:\n```python\ndef line_of(path: Path, value, key=None) -> int | None:\n    try:\n        lines = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines()\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    vs = str(value)\n    hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(lines, 1) if vs in ln and (key is None or f'\"{key}\"' in ln)]\n    return hits[0] if hits else None\n```\n\nBut for frozenAt in custody.json, value appears in `\"frozenAt\": \"...\"`. Fine.\n\nLet me think about which source to prefer. The bar N3 evaluator probably just needs a file:line where the time appears. So either custody.json or custody-events.jsonl works as long as the line carries the time. The task's Gap 1 says \"each time with an x-plumbline source naming file and line\" — file can be the harness log or ledger.\n\nSo for approvals:\n```python\nsrc = None\nif live:\n    src = f\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:{live['_line']}\"\nelif a.get(\"at\"):\n    n = line_of(inputs_path, a[\"at\"], \"at\")\n    if n: src = f\"{inputs_path.name}:{n}\"\nxa[\"at_source\"] = src\n```\n\nWait, `inputs_path.name` is \"custody.json\", but path relative to job is \"inputs/custody.json\". Let me use relative path. Since inputs_path is `job / \"inputs\" / \"custody.json\"`, `inputs_path.relative_to(job)` = \"inputs/custody.json\". Good.\n\nFor freeze:\n```python\nfz = ev_log.get(\"freeze\")\nif fz:\n    ... xp[\"bar_frozenAt_source\"] = f\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:{fz['_line']}\"\nelse:\n    n = line_of(inputs_path, b.get(\"frozenAt\"), \"frozenAt\")\n    ...\n```\n\nWhere to put the source? The bar N3 says \"an x-plumbline source\". Let me put a consolidated map under `predicate.x-plumbline.sources` and also individual sources where natural.\n\nLet me define `xp[\"sources\"] = {}` and populate:\n- `sources[\"bar.frozenAt\"] = ...`\n- `sources[f\"bar.approvals[{i}].at\"] = ...`\n- `sources[\"startedAt\"] = ...`\n- `sources[\"producedAt\"] = ...`\n- `sources[\"disposition.at\"] = ...`\n- For checks, per-check `x-plumbline.at_source`.\n\nHmm, but N3 says \"Every time field ... has an x-plumbline source\". A per-check x-plumbline is an x-plumbline source. A predicate-level map is also x-plumbline. To be safe, do both: per-object `x-plumbline.at_source` and a top-level `sources` map.\n\nWait, but for approvals, the x-plumbline already has `time_source` = \"logged live (...)\". I can add `at_source` = file:line.\n\nLet me now also handle disposition. Currently:\n```python\nif live:\n    at = live.get(\"at\")\n...\nelif live:\n    xp[\"disposition_at_source\"] = \"logged live (inputs/custody-events.jsonl)\"\n```\nAdd `xp[\"disposition_at_line\"]` or `xp[\"sources\"][\"disposition.at\"]`.\n\nLet me now think about Gap 2.\n\n### Gap 2 (N4-N6)\n\nN4: On `genreply-null` (a model pass whose `/api/v1/generation` reply is missing/null), the emitter either (a) exits non-zero with a named error, or (b) records that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE AND writes an x-plumbline flag naming the pass. It never writes an endpoint no reply evidences.\n\nN5: On `genreply-full`, N4's procedure passes and each endpoint equals the reply's provider host.\n\nN6: The generation-reply code path in `kit/emit_custody.py` has no fallback that derives an endpoint from routing config or provider pins. Answer CONFORMS/DOES_NOT_CONFORM + cited line numbers.\n\nWait, N6 says the generation-reply code path in `kit/emit_custody.py` has no fallback. But the endpoint logic is mostly in `custody_history.py` (`endpoint_of`). Hmm. N6 is evaluated by a model (E-GLM) reading `kit/emit_custody.py` and citing line numbers. So the relevant code path might be in emit_custody.py? Let me look.\n\nIn `emit_custody.py`, `model_block` sets endpoint from inputs. In `build`, for maker:\n```python\nm_model = dict(m.get(\"model\") or {}, **({\"endpoint\": mk[\"endpoint\"]} if mk.get(\"endpoint\") else {}))\n```\nSo if `mk[\"endpoint\"]` is None, it keeps `m.get(\"model\")[\"endpoint\"]` (hand-entered). That's a fallback to routing config/provider pins! N6 says no fallback that derives an endpoint from routing config or provider pins.\n\nHmm, wait: `m.get(\"model\").get(\"endpoint\")` is hand-entered in custody.json, not \"routing config or provider pins\". But the task says \"never derive an `endpoint` from routing config or provider pins\". The hand-entered endpoint in custody.json might be considered a pin/config. Actually, the endpoint in custody.json's model block is likely typed from the routing config. So keeping it when the generation reply is null is a fallback.\n\nLet me look at `model_block`:\n```python\nfor k in (\"family\", \"identifier\", \"endpoint\", \"readOnly\"):\n    if k in m and m[k] not in (None, \"\"):\n        out[k] = m[k]\n```\nSo if `m[\"model\"]` has `endpoint`, it's written. In `build`, `m_model = dict(m.get(\"model\") or {}, **({\"endpoint\": mk[\"endpoint\"]} if mk.get(\"endpoint\") else {}))`. If `mk[\"endpo