is an aggregator call?), then refuse (exit 3) with a named error, OR mark external evidence absent and omit endpoint, with a flag.\n\nBut wait, N5 requires on `genreply-full` that the procedure passes and endpoints equal the reply's provider host. So when generation is present, endpoint = reply's provider host. The reply's provider host — how to get a host from the generation reply? The generation reply has `provider_name` but not a host. Hmm. \"each endpoint equals the reply's provider host\". The reply's provider host... Maybe the endpoint is derived from the provider name mapping? But we don't have a mapping. Or maybe \"the reply's provider host\" means the host of the provider named in the reply, which is the pin host if the reply names the pinned provider.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The generation reply (GET /api/v1/generation) returns `data.provider_name` and maybe `data.provider_responses`. It doesn't return a host. So \"the reply's provider host\" likely means: the endpoint is the host the Orchestrator declared for the provider named in the reply. I.e., pin.host, but only if the reply's provider_name matches the pin. So N5: endpoint = pin.host (because reply names the pinned provider). And N4: if reply is null, can't confirm, so don't use pin.host.\n\nSo the requirement is: only derive endpoint from pin if the generation reply (or response.provider?) evidences the provider. Specifically, the generation reply is required evidence. N4 says \"no /api/v1/generation reply\" → never derive endpoint from pin. So `served_by` from `response.provider` alone is not enough; generation is required.\n\nHmm, but `served_by` reads response.provider AND generation. `endpoint_of` checks `c.served` non-empty. If response.provider is present but generation is null, `c.served` is non-empty, so endpoint_of returns pin.host. That violates N4.\n\nSo I need to change the logic: for aggregator calls, require `generation` to be present (non-null) to confirm served_by and derive endpoint. If generation is null, either refuse or mark CL1-15 absent and omit endpoint.\n\nLet me re-read N4 carefully: \"On fixture `genreply-null` (a model pass whose `/api/v1/generation` reply is missing/null) the emitter either (a) exits non-zero with a named error, or (b) records that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE **and** writes an `x-plumbline` flag naming the pass. It never writes an `endpoint` no reply evidences.\"\n\n\"that evaluator's CL1-15 evidence absent\" — so the evaluator's external evidence should be absent. check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE for CL1-15.\n\nSo option (b): for the evaluator with a null generation reply, set external evidence to None (absent), so check.py reads NOT-CHECKABLE for CL1-15. And add x-plumbline flag naming the pass. And don't write endpoint.\n\nBut the external evidence is built from `ci.get(\"external\")`, which names an evidence_file (the harness log). Even if generation is null, the harness log exists and is named. If we keep it, check.py would read OK (evidence present). To make check.py read NOT-CHECKABLE, we must remove the external entry's evidence (set to None).\n\nHmm, but check.py's CL1-15 check: \"For every model evaluator, `external` holds evidence of its configuration — for example a harness log showing which model and endpoint answered\". If external evidence is a harness log but that log doesn't show the generation reply (endpoint), is it valid? The checker might just check presence of evidence. To force NOT-CHECKABLE, set evidence to None.\n\nSo for genreply-null, we should:\n- For the evaluator whose call has null generation, set its external evidence to None.\n- Add x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\n- Don't write endpoint (leave it absent).\n\nAlternatively, option (a): exit non-zero with named error. That's simpler: if any model call lacks generation, add a refusal (exit 3) with a named error. But N4 says \"exits non-zero with a named error\" — exit 3 is non-zero. That would make the fixture fail to emit, which the evaluator records as pass (exit code non-zero). But then check.py wouldn't run (no record). The bar says \"evidence/N4.json (exit code, stderr, record diff, check.py output)\". If no record, check.py output is empty. That might be acceptable under option (a).\n\nBut wait, if we refuse, the fixture `genreply-null` never emits a record. The evaluator might still pass N4(a). But then N5 (genreply-full) must pass, and N3 etc. for genreply-full.\n\nHmm, but the task says \"refuse with a named error or record the CL1-15 evidence absent with an x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\" Either is acceptable. The simplest and least likely to break other things is option (a): refuse with a named error. But would that break existing tests? The existing failure fixtures don't include genreply-null. So refusing on null generation only affects new behavior. But wait — do any existing fixtures have null generation? job-rejected's harness log: if it was generated by or_call and the generation fetch succeeded, it has generation. If not... Hmm.\n\nActually, the base code's `served_by` uses response.provider etc. If job-rejected's log has response.provider but no generation, and I now require generation, then job-rejected would refuse, breaking existing tests. That's a big risk.\n\nLet me look at the fixture description again: \"Fixture job = a job dir under tests/fixtures/custody/ with inputs/custody-events.jsonl, harness logs and an inputs/custody.json.\" The existing job-rejected likely has harness logs. If those logs were written by or_call with generation, fine. If they were hand-written synthetic logs without generation, then requiring generation would break them.\n\nWe can't see the fixture files. Hmm. But the base code's docstring mentions `/api/v1/generation` stats as one of the sources. The test suite at base passes with job-rejected. Does job-rejected's log have generation? The failure fixture `pin-provider-missing` says \"the log names no serving provider (no `provider`, metadata or generation)\". So that fixture's log has no provider info. But job-rejected presumably has serving provider info (otherwise it would refuse). Could be response.provider without generation.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, let me choose a targeted approach:\n- For maker and evaluator calls, if the call went to an aggregator and the log does NOT have a `generation` field at all (missing key) vs. `generation: null` (present but null)?\n  - The harness `or_call.py` always sets `entry[\"generation\"]` to the reply or None. So `generation` key present, value null means the fetch failed.\n  - Hand-written fixtures might omit `generation` entirely.\n- The requirement is specifically about \"no /api/v1/generation reply\" (missing/null). So if `generation` is absent or null, we should not derive endpoint from pin.\n\nBut to avoid breaking job-rejected if it lacks generation, maybe job-rejected uses direct provider calls (not aggregator), so endpoint = request URL host, no pin needed. The `job-rejected` fixture likely calls providers directly (URL host = endpoint). Then generation is irrelevant. Let me check the README: \"one model evaluator, one batch\". It doesn't mention OpenRouter. The base fixture probably uses direct calls to avoid pin complexity. The failure overlays `router-endpoint`, `pin-*` add aggregator calls. So job-rejected's logs likely have direct URLs (e.g., `https://api.deepseek.com/...`), not openrouter.ai. Then `endpoint_of` returns `c.host` directly, no generation needed.\n\nSo requiring generation only for aggregator calls would not affect job-rejected. Good.\n\nBut the held-out `genreply-null` fixture is presumably an aggregator call (OpenRouter) with generation null. For that, we must not derive endpoint from pin.\n\nSo the rule: for an aggregator call, `served_by` must be confirmed by the generation reply (not just response.provider). If generation is null/absent, refuse or mark absent.\n\nLet me look at `endpoint_of`:\n```python\nif not c.served:\n    ref.add(\"endpoint\", ... \"the log does not say which provider served it ...\")\n    return None, route\n```\n`c.served` includes response.provider. So if response.provider present but generation null, it proceeds. I need to make `served_by` require generation for aggregator calls, or add a check.\n\nOption: In `served_by`, only include `response.provider` if generation is present? No, that changes N5 too. Let me add a separate flag `c.has_generation` and in `endpoint_of`, for aggregator calls, require generation to be present:\n```python\nif c.host in AGGREGATORS and not c.generation:\n    ref.add(\"endpoint\", f\"{who}: {c.label} ... has no /api/v1/generation reply ...\")\n    return None, route\n```\nBut that's a refusal (exit 3), which is option (a). Then the fixture genreply-null would refuse with a named error. That satisfies N4(a). And N6: no fallback. Good.\n\nBut wait, the task says for Gap 2: \"refuse with a named error or record the CL1-15 evidence absent with an x-plumbline flag naming the pass.\" Refusing is acceptable.\n\nBut hold on — would refusing break the held-out `genreply-null` fixture's expected behavior? The bar says N4 PASS when either (a) exits non-zero with a named error, or (b) records absent. So refusing passes. Good.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: N4 also says \"It never writes an endpoint no reply evidences.\" If we refuse, nothing is written, so no endpoint. Good.\n\nBut what about `genreply-full`? All replies present, so no refusal, endpoint derived from pin (confirmed by generation). N5 requires \"each endpoint equals the reply's provider host\". Hmm, \"the reply's provider host\". If the generation reply has `provider_name` = \"DeepInfra\", the \"provider host\" is the host of DeepInfra, which is the pin host `api.deepinfra.com`. So endpoint = pin.host. Good.\n\nBut wait, does the generation reply contain a host? Let me check OpenRouter's generation response fields. It has `provider_name`, not