e calls filtered out, `has_any` on filtered. Fine.\n\nNow, let me think about `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)`. If `started` = sent_at and custody.json's startedAt is typed to created (server) and they differ >60s (possible if generation took long? created is when generation began on server, which is after request sent; could be seconds, not 600s). For maker-10min, first call's sent_at and created are close. So fine.\n\nNow, let me think about `check_order`. It uses `S = P(pred.get(\"startedAt\"))`, `Pd = P(pred.get(\"producedAt\"))`. With sent_at/received_at, ordering: frozenAt < startedAt <= producedAt <= checks. For maker-10min, startedAt is 600s before producedAt. Checks happen after producedAt? The evaluator runs after the maker. So checks after producedAt. Good. But with sent_at (earlier than created), startedAt is even earlier, so frozenAt < startedAt still holds (freeze before maker start). Good.\n\nFor the base job-rejected, if startedAt now = sent_at (earlier than created), and frozenAt was just before created, could frozenAt > startedAt? If freeze happened after the maker's request was sent? Unlikely; freeze is before maker starts. But if the fixture's freeze time is between sent_at and created... For a single call, sent_at and created differ by network latency (small). The fixture's frozenAt is probably minutes before. Fine.\n\nNow, `check_order` also checks approvals before startedAt:\n```python\nbefore(n, t, \"startedAt (the maker's first call)\", S)\n```\nApprovals must be before startedAt. With startedAt = sent_at (earlier), approvals still before. Fine.\n\nNow, let me consider `after-emit` check: `from_log = xp[\"times\"]`. If startedAt from harness log, tolerance 60s. Fine.\n\nNow, let me think about `route_x` and `x[\"startedAt_basis\"]`. Update.\n\nLet me now write the changes to `maker_history`:\n```python\ndef maker_history(job: Path, ev_dir: Path, ci: dict, m: dict, ref: H.Refusals, pin: dict | None = None,\n                  gaps: Gaps | None = None) -> dict:\n    log = find_log(...)\n    ...\n    calls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")\n    if m.get(\"calls\"):\n        calls = [c for c in calls if c.name in m[\"calls\"]]\n    calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]\n    if not calls:\n        return {}\n    annotate(calls, \"maker\", (m.get(\"model\") or {}).get(\"identifier\"), ref, pin)\n    check_pin_order(pin, calls, \"maker\", ref)\n    first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)\n    last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)\n    started, produced = first.started, last.at\n    H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, f\"harness log {first.label}\")\n    H.cross_check(ref, \"producedAt\", ci.get(\"producedAt\"), produced, f\"harness log {last.label}\")\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, `first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` where `started` = sent or created or date. For the first call (chronologically), sent_at is earliest. For the second call, sent_at later. So min picks first. Good. But what if a later call has an earlier sent_at due to clock weirdness? Unlikely.\n\n`last = max(calls, key=lambda c: c.at)` where `at` = received or date or created. Picks the latest received_at. Good.\n\nNow, add the gap enforcement:\n```python\n    if gaps is not None and any(\"sent_at\" in c.raw or \"received_at\" in c.raw for c in calls):\n        for c in calls:\n            missing = [k for k in (\"sent_at\", \"received_at\") if not c.raw.get(k)]\n            if missing:\n                gaps.add(f\"maker.{c.label}\", f\"the Maker harness call {c.label} carries no \"\n                          f\"{' or '.join(missing)}; startedAt/producedAt cannot be read from the log\")\n```\n\nHmm, `not c.raw.get(k)` treats empty string as missing. Fine.\n\nNow, the `x`:\n```python\n    out = {\"startedAt\": H.iso(started), \"producedAt\": H.iso(produced), \"endpoint\": last.endpoint,\n           \"x\": {**route_x(log, calls),\n                 \"startedAt_basis\": f\"{fir