substring, `2026-...:...Z` is not a substring of `2026-...:...123456Z`. Hmm! For held-out fixtures, N3 currently fails only due to the key. After we add `timeSources`, if the instrument does exact substring, it would still fail because of microseconds.\n\nWait, the evaluator's N3 finding for held-out is \"no time field names a {file, line} source in x-plumbline\". It didn't get to the \"line carries that time\" check because no sources. After we add sources, it will check the line. If it does exact substring, held-out fails due to microseconds. So we need to handle this.\n\nOptions:\n1. Make the record's times include microseconds exactly as logged. Then the line contains the exact string. But `H.iso` truncates. We could change `iso` to preserve microseconds when present. But then the record's time format changes; schema allows fractional seconds? The custody schema likely uses `format: date-time` which allows fractional seconds. The Plumbline record schema might too. But `check_order` and `cross_check` parse with `RFC` regex allowing `(\\.\\d+)?`. Fine. N1 compares at whole seconds. N2 difference 600s. Fine.\n   - But `H.iso(d)` is used for many derived times (emission, disposition, checks). If we change `iso` globally, emission time gets microseconds, which is fine. But the docs examples show whole seconds. And tests might assert exact `...Z` without fractional. Risky.\n   - Alternatively, only for startedAt/producedAt, use the raw `sent_at`/`received_at` string (with microseconds) instead of `H.iso`. Then the record's startedAt matches the line exactly. But the record's startedAt would have microseconds while other times don't. The custody schema allows. N1 compares at whole seconds. `cross_check` parses. `check_order` parses. `ts()` accepts. Hmm, but `mk[\"startedAt\"]` is used in `ts(mk.get(\"startedAt\") or ci.get(\"startedAt\"), ...)`. If we set `mk[\"startedAt\"]` to the raw string with microseconds, the record has it. Good.\n   - But `started` is a datetime