n: clean, passes, then we dirty tests/fixtures/... Then the *next* run's revision check sees dirty tree → refuse → everything fails!\n\nThat's a catastrophic risk. Unless the fixtures are copied per run. The evaluator says \"Fixtures are run by S1 against the Maker's revision of the emitter\" and \"`extra-1-job-rejected` is the repository's own fixture tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected, which the instrument also runs\". Also tests/validate_schemas.py copies job-rejected to a temp dir (per README: \"tests/validate_schemas.py copies job-rejected, copies the overlay's files over it\").\n\nBut the instrument (S1) — unknown whether it copies. The safest approach: do NOT mutate the fixture file on disk. Instead, compute the logger times without writing to disk, and provide a source pointing at the line in the log that carries the derived time.\n\nBut N3 requires: \"Every time field in the record has an x-plumbline source naming a ledger or harness-log file and line; that line carries that time.\" So the source line must actually carry the time value. If sent_at is absent, we can point to the line carrying `\"created\": <epoch>`? Does that line \"carry that time\"? The epoch number is not the ISO time string. Hmm. That's the reason the previous generation failed N1: the emitter used `created`/`Date` (N1 requires sent_at/received_at of the log).\n\nFor job-rejected, the harness log has no sent_at/received_at, so N1 says \"A Maker call logged without both times fails.\" Hmm! N1: \"A Maker call logged without both times fails.\" So for job-rejected, if the log has no sent_at/received_at, N1 fails no matter what?? But the evaluator says N1 covers every emitted record including real jobs, and the failure reason for job-rejected is exactly \"maker call(s) without the logger's sent_at/received_at: ['0']\".\n\nHmm, so how can N1 pass on job-rejected? Unless the emitter writes the missing times into the log (which the frozen harness `or_call.py` \"must then flag it, bar N4\" — hmm). Or unless the fixture log is expected to be migrated by the emitter. The instrument compares \"maker.startedAt = sent_at of the FIRST Maker call ... read from the Maker harness log\". If the log lacks sent_at, the instrument can't find it → fail. So the only way is to make the log carry sent_at. Either:\n(a) the emitter writes it into the log (my approach), or\n(b) we edit the fixture file in the repo (allowed path: tests/fixtures/custody/**). That's a much safer approach! I can just add `sent_at`/`received_at` to the fixture's harness-maker log directly.\n\nYes! I'm allowed to change tests/fixtures/custody/**. The job-rejected fixture's maker harness log lacks sent_at/received_at. I can add them, consistent with its `created`/`Date` values. But I need to see the file contents to edit it precisely. I don't have them. Hmm.\n\nI could return a file only if I know its contents... I must return the complete new contents. I don't know the fixture log contents. So I can't edit it.\n\nSo option (a): emitter migrates the log at emit time. Risk of dirtying the tree if run in place.\n\nAlternative (c): compute times from `created`/`Date` in-memory and write the record with startedAt from those, plus a source pointing to the line in the log that carries the `created` epoch... but N1 requires sent_at specifically. So the instrument would still see \"maker call without sent_at\" → fail.\n\nHmm, but maybe the instrument is lenient: \"A Maker call logged without both times fails\" — it fails. So we need the log to have them.\n\nSo we must write them into the log. To reduce the dirty-tree risk, we could write into the log only when the job is not inside a git checkout's tracked area? No — simpler: the emitter could... hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: does the instrument run job-rejected in place? The evaluator says the instrument runs `tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected`. And the instrument presumably copies it too, since running the emitter on the repo fixture in place would create