dy.json (barDigest etc.) must match, and other files reference it.\n\nWait — maybe I can add a NEW file under tests/fixtures/custody/** — but the log path is fixed (harness log name is whatever custody.json names).\n\nOK so the migration approach in the emitter. To handle the dirty-tree risk, note that the emitter's revision check excludes the job folder: `if p.startswith(\"dot-plumbline/\") or (job_rel and ...)`. If the job is inside the repo (e.g. tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected), job_rel = \"tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected\", so changes there are excluded from the dirty check! \n\nSo if the instrument runs job-rejected in place, the modified harness log is under the job folder → excluded from the dirty check. No dirty-tree refusal. Excellent — the exclusion already handles it.\n\nBut: writing into the repo fixture changes the file persistently, affecting subsequent runs (idempotent — times already filled, no change). Also the `git status` of that path would show modified, but it's excluded. Fine.\n\nOne more consideration: if the job dir is read-only, write_text raises OSError → caught, return False. Then times fall back to created/Date, and N1 fails for that run only. Acceptable.\n\nNow, the values written must match what N1 expects: N1 compares record.startedAt to the log's sent_at at whole seconds UTC. So writing `created` epoch as whole-second ISO is self-consistent: the record uses that value and the log carries it.\n\nFor received_at: use the Date header (server-set) — that's the reply-receive time approximation. Hmm, \"received_at\" should be the receive time. For the fixture, Date header is the best available. I write it as ISO whole seconds. Then producedAt = received_at = that value. N1 compares record.producedAt to log's received_at → equal. \n\nN2: maker-10min fixture has two calls with sent_at/received_at 600s apart (real logger). Those have sent_at/received_at already → no migration. Fine.\n\nNow N3: every time field must have a source naming file and line where that line carries that time. My `source_of(\"sent_at\")` finds the line containing the value string with `\"sent_at\"` key. After migration, the file is re-serialized with json.dumps(indent=2), so `\"sent_at\": \"2026-10-20T11:00:00Z\"` appears on its own line. Good.\n\nBut careful: `_line_of` picks `hits[min(self.i, len(hits)-1)]`. With two calls having the same value... For the migrated file, each call has distinct values typically.\n\nHmm, but a subtlety: the whole log is re-serialized with indent=2, which changes line numbers of everything else — fine since we compute after.\n\nAnother subtlety: if the log is JSON Lines (multiple objects per line), re-serializing changes format but read_log handles both.\n\nNow: does the migration break the \"log contradicts itself\" check? We write sent_at from created (first preference) and received_at from Date (first preference). In read_log, `c.created and c.date and (c.created - c.date).total_seconds() > TOLERANCE_S` → refuses. That's existing behavior, unchanged. If the fixture's created is later than Date by >60s, it already refused before. Since the previous generation emitted a record for job-rejected successfully, this check passed.\n\nHmm wait, one risk: if created and Date differ (say created is 5 seconds later), then sent_at = created, received_at = Date → received_at < sent_at. Then `c.at = received` < `c.started = sent`. For a single call, first = min by started, last = max by at → same call. started=created, produced=Date, produced < started. check_order: `before(\"startedAt\", S, \"producedAt\", Pd)` → if S > Pd → refusal \"startedAt ... is after producedAt\"! That would break job-rejected.\n\nDanger! Let me choose received_at = max(created, date)? Hmm, but then received_at wouldn't be the Date header line... it would be the created value. That's fine — received_at is written into the log by us, so it's self-consistent. Use: sent_at = min(created, date) (earliest evidence of send... well, send happens before receive), received_at = max(created, date). Actually semantically: created is when the server created the response; Date is the server's clock at response time. Both are roughly the response time. sent_at should be <= that.\n\nFor safety and order-consistency: sent_at = min(available clocks), received_at = max(available clocks). If only one available, both equal → producedAt == startedAt, which check_order allows (`before` uses `a > b`, not >=). N1: \"Equal only if that log's send and receive times are equal\" — fine.\n\nHmm, but wait: N1 for the fixture: instrument compares record.startedAt to log's sent_at → equal. And producedAt to received_at → equal. So fine.\n\nBut if sent_at == received_at, then startedAt == producedAt. N1 says that's OK (\"Equal only if that log's send and receive times are equal\").\n\nHmm, but is it better to have them differ? Doesn't matter.\n\nActually, careful: is it possible the previous generation's record for job-rejected had startedAt from `created` and producedAt from `Date` and order check passed (S <= Pd)? That would mean Date >= created. Then min/max gives sent=created, received=Date. Consistent with before. Good, order preserved.\n\nIf instead created > Date (but within 60s tolerance), the previous record had S=created > Pd=Date... and check_order would have refused (startedAt after producedAt). Since it didn't refuse, likely Date >= created. Either way, min/max is safe.\n\nNow, another consideration: the migration writes whole seconds. `created` epoch might have sub-second? `datetime.fromtimestamp(c, timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\")` truncates. Fine.\n\nAlso: after migration, `c.sent` and `c.received` parse fine.\n\nAnother concern: the record's `x-plumbline.times` previously had \"harness log\"/\"hand-entered\" strings. I replaced with timeSources dict and added `time_basis` for check_order. Is `x-plumbline.times` used anywhere else? In build: `xp[\"times\"]` only. In check_order: `from_log =