repo. \"extra-1-job-rejected is the repository's own fixture tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected, which the instrument also runs\". So the instrument runs the emitter on the repo fixture directly (maybe in a copy of the repo). It then reads the maker harness log — the repo one. If the emitter rewrites it in place during that run, the instrument reads the rewritten file → sees sent_at → passes N1. And the tree-dirtiness problem: the record is emitted... the emitter checks revision BEFORE writing the log (revision() is called early in build()). The dirty check happens at build start. Then we write the log during maker_history — after the check. So the record still gets written (revision was checked before the write). Subsequent emits in the same job would then be dirty-refused... but for job-rejected the instrument probably emits once.\n\nBut in the test suite, multiple emits happen on copies. If a copy is inside the checkout, later emits from that copy see a dirty tree → refusal → test failure. Hmm, but tests presumably use `--expect-revision` with a throwaway commit... The docs say \"the emitter runs from a throwaway clean commit of the tree\" — so tests copy the tree to a temp dir and commit it. Then writing into the copy's fixtures dir dirties the throwaway tree. If a later test uses the same throwaway commit for another emit and doesn't expect dirtiness → failure.\n\nGiven the crash we saw, the safest is: don't write in place, period.\n\nAlternative: write the migrated log to a NEW file (never overwriting), e.g. `<job>/custody/evidence/harness-maker-fixture.log.migrated.log`? Still inside the job → if the job is in the repo, dirty tree. Same problem. Unless we write it under dot-plumbline (excluded) — but the job isn't necessarily under dot-plumbline.\n\nHmm. Wait, actually: the revision dirty check excludes the job folder itself: `if p.startswith(\"dot-plumbline/\") or (job_rel and (p == job_rel or p.startswith(job_rel + \"/\")))`. So writing INSIDE THE JOB FOLD