created if no date), `at` = received_at = date → same value. Good, identical.\n\nAnd `started = sent or created or date`; before: created if present else date. After: sent_at = created if created else date. Same. \n\nSo preserving shape and injecting times equal to the values already used keeps every derived time identical. That's the minimal-risk change.\n\nNow, does the test suite depend on the harness log's exact bytes? Possibly `MANIFEST` in dot-plumbline (not touched). Probably not.\n\nBut another concern: the in-place write happens during test runs on copies — fine. If it happens on the repo fixture directly, the file in the repo changes (dirty tree), but tests run from a throwaway copy per the docs (\"the emitter runs from a throwaway clean commit of the tree\"). Hmm, but the emitter checks `revision` dirtiness of the repo tree — if the emitter writes into tests/fixtures/..., that's outside dot-plumbline and would make the tree dirty for subsequent emits → refusals! That's a real risk.\n\nWait, but the test says the emitter runs from a throwaway clean commit of the tree, so writes to the checkout... Hmm. If the emitter writes into `tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected/custody/evidence/harness-maker-fixture.log` inside the checkout, then `git status` becomes dirty → subsequent emit calls would refuse with `[revision] tree is dirty`. That would break the whole suite.\n\nHmm, but does the emit smoke run on the fixture in-place? The failure occurred at line 586 which is after... we don't know. But the crash proves that at line 586, `x` was a list — meaning the log at that path had been rewritten. Which means the emitter DID write to the path the test then read. If that path is inside the repo checkout, the tree got dirty.\n\nSo in-place writing is definitely too dangerous. Must not write into the job's evidence dir if it's inside the repo.\n\nHmm. But then how to satisfy N1 for job-rejected?\n\nUnless... the evaluator's fixture job is a *copy* placed outside the r