that covers all sources including generation. Good.\n\nSo for a log where response.provider = \"Together\" and no generation → my code refuses earlier with \"carries no /api/v1/generation reply\". The message differs from base. If the test asserts the exact message for `pin-fallback`... the fixture overlay `pin-fallback` provides its own log which presumably includes a generation reply naming Together. The base code's message for that overlay: \"was served by 'Together' (response provider), 'Together' (generation provider_name), not the pinned provider...\" — from the docs example! Yes, the docs show exactly that message for pin-fallback. So that overlay's log HAS a generation reply naming Together. My code: has_generation ✓, generation_names_provider ✓ (Together), then `other` includes both sources → message: \"was served by 'Together' (response provider), 'Together' (generation provider_name), not the pinned provider 'DeepInfra' (deepinfra/fp8): a fallback to another provider; the record would name api.deepinfra.com for a call it did not answer\" — matches the docs example exactly (ordering: dict order is response provider, openrouter_metadata..., then generation; my `served_by` updates with generation after, preserving insertion order → \"response provider\" first, then \"generation provider_name\"). \n\nBut line 586 is in the test file: `x[\"response\"][\"openrouter_metadata\"] = {\"attempts\": [{\"provider\": \"Together\", ...}]}`. That's the test constructing an overlay inline — probably the `pin-provider-missing` variant or a \"tried fallback\" case. It sets attempts (tried), not served. So served stays as the pin, but `tried` contains Together → base refuses with \"was also sent to 'Together'...\". My code: needs generation present. If this inline case has a generation reply naming the pin → passes to the `tried` check → same message. If no generation → my code refuses earlier with a different message.\n\nHmm. The test at 586 — since it's modifying `x[\"response\"]`, `x` is a call dict in the log. The test likely then writes the log and runs the emitter, expecting refusal kind \"endpoint\".\n\nGiven uncertainty, the risk is: my stricter requirement (generation must name provider) changes messages/kinds for these inline test cases. Kinds stay \"endpoint\" in all these paths, so a kind-based assertion passes. A message-substring assertion might fail.\n\nHmm, what could the test assert? Let me think about the structure of validate_schemas.py part 3: \"every refusal above (synthetic failure fixtures...)\". Probably it checks exit code 3 and that stderr mentions a kind like `[endpoint]`. Possibly it checks specific expected kinds per overlay (as the README table lists). The inline case at 586 is likely an extra check not in the table.\n\nI'll accept the risk but minimize: make the \"generation reply must name the provider\" requirement only bite when it matters, while keeping all other refusals identical. Specifically: if the generation reply is present but names no provider, we refuse — that's needed for N6. But if the generation reply is present and names a provider, behavior is as base.\n\nThe inline case at 586: if the fixture log's calls have no `generation` key at all, then with my change any aggregator call refuses with \"carries no /api/v1/generation reply\". Does the fixture `job-rejected` maker log have a generation? It must, because base code requires `c.has_generation` for aggregator calls, and job-rejected emits successfully (exit 1, record written). Unless the fixture's maker goes direct (not through OpenRouter) — then no pin and no generation needed.\n\nHmm! If job-rejected's maker log is a direct call (no aggregator), then `x[\"response\"][\"openrouter_metadata\"]` mutation at line 586 wouldn't matter for endpoint... Actually it might: adding attempts to a direct call → base code ignores (not aggregator). Hmm, then why set it?\n\nWait — maybe at line 586 the test isn't about the maker but builds a *new* log 