e\"].get(a.get(\"role\"))` → if live exists, sets `at_source` to ledger line. Good.\n- disposition: `live = ev_log.get(\"dispose\")` → if live and at matches, sets source to ledger line. Good.\n\nSo if we add the events to the fixture's ledger, the emitter will cite them. But wait — the `events_log` function reads events; for approve it maps by role. For freeze, it stores `out[e[\"event\"]] = e`. For dispose, `out[\"dispose\"] = e`. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the ledger events must match the hand-entered times, or the emitter refuses (`ref.add(\"time\", ...)`). So we add events with exactly those times.\n\nAlso for `pin` the fixture already has a pin event.\n\nNow, but the N3 instrument checks ALL fixture folders under tests/fixtures/custody/ that have inputs/custody.json. That includes `job-rejected` and maybe overlays in `failures/`. Let's check: `failures/README.md` says each folder is an overlay on `../job-rejected`. Do failure overlay folders contain `inputs/custody.json`? They likely contain partial overlays (only changed files). The instrument \"runs every folder under tests/fixtures/custody/ that has inputs/custody.json\". If a failure overlay has its own `inputs/custody.json` (full copy), then it would also be checked. But failure overlays are expected to be refused (exit 3), so no record written → N3 not checked. Except `routing-unpinned` which is exit 1 (record written). Hmm, does routing-unpinned have inputs/custody.json? Possibly. Let me consider.\n\nActually the findings only mention `extra-1-job-rejected` failing, and say \"every time field on the held-out fixtures PASSes.\" So the held-out fixtures already have ledger events. The only failing run is job-rejected. So we just need to fix job-rejected (and any other fixture with the same issue that might be run).\n\nWait, but the held-out fixtures are \"shaped like tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected\" and \"their Maker and evaluator harness logs are written by the harness\". The held-out fixtures